window.LING285_PLAIN = {"concepts": {"directions": {"opening": "**A mass-spring system can vibrate in two directions, and both directions work the same way.** In **longitudinal motion**, the mass moves back and forth along the spring. In **transverse motion**, the mass moves up and down across the system, between stretched supports.", "mechanism": "When the mass moves away from **equilibrium** (its resting position), a **restoring force** pulls it back. Because of **inertia**, the moving mass keeps going past equilibrium, so it overshoots and gets pulled back again. In the transverse system, the **tension** (pulling force) in the stretched supports supplies the restoring force.", "consequence": "So only the direction of the motion changes between the two systems. **The restoring force and the overshoot stay the same.**", "plain": "Along the spring or across it, the system pulls the mass back and the mass overshoots.", "q1": {"question": "How can longitudinal and transverse vibration work the same way?", "answer": "**Both directions combine a restoring force with inertia.** The restoring force pulls the mass back toward equilibrium. Because of inertia, the mass keeps moving past equilibrium, so it overshoots. Changing the direction of the motion does not change that interaction."}, "q2": {"question": "What changes between the two directions, and what stays the same?", "answer": "**The direction of the motion changes, but the restoring force and the overshoot stay the same.** Longitudinal motion goes along the system, and transverse motion goes across it. In both, the system pulls the mass back and inertia carries it past equilibrium."}}, "mass-stiffness": {"opening": "**In a mass-spring system, more mass lowers the frequency, and more stiffness raises it.** **Frequency** (pitch) is the number of complete cycles in one second. Each rule holds only when the other property stays the same.", "mechanism": "More mass is harder to speed up and turn around, so each cycle takes longer. A stiffer spring pulls back harder for the same stretch, so the mass returns more quickly.", "consequence": "So a prediction has to say which property changed and which one stayed the same. If mass and stiffness both change, you have to weigh both effects together.", "plain": "Heavier means slower and lower, and stiffer means faster and higher.", "q1": {"question": "Why does more mass lower the frequency while more stiffness raises it?", "answer": "**More mass slows the system down, and more stiffness speeds it up.** More mass has more inertia, so it is harder to turn around and each cycle takes longer. A stiffer spring pulls back harder, so the mass returns sooner and the frequency goes up."}, "q2": {"question": "Why do you have to say what stayed the same when you predict a frequency change?", "answer": "**Because mass and stiffness push the frequency in opposite directions.** If both change at once, their effects compete. So you have to know what stayed the same before you can use one rule."}}, "two-modes": {"opening": "**Two masses joined by springs have two normal modes, and Mode 2 is faster.** A **normal mode** is one organized pattern of motion of the whole system. In **Mode 1**, the two masses move together. In **Mode 2**, they move in opposite directions.", "mechanism": "When the masses move together, the middle spring is hardly stretched. When they move in opposite directions, the middle spring and the outer springs all stretch. So the restoring force is stronger and **the frequency is higher**.", "consequence": "So a mode is a pattern of the whole system, not a frequency that belongs to one mass. Both masses take part in each mode, and the two modes can combine into more complicated motion.", "plain": "Moving together is Mode 1 and slower, and moving opposite is Mode 2 and faster.", "q1": {"question": "How are the two normal modes of a two-mass system different?", "answer": "**In Mode 1 the two masses move together, and in Mode 2 they move in opposite directions.** Each mode is a pattern of the whole system, so both masses take part in both modes. Mode 2 has the higher frequency."}, "q2": {"question": "Why does Mode 2 have a higher frequency than Mode 1?", "answer": "**Mode 2 is faster because more springs are stretching.** When the masses move in opposite directions, the middle spring stretches a lot. That adds restoring force, so the masses turn around sooner and the frequency goes up."}}, "three-modes": {"opening": "**Three masses joined by springs have three normal modes, ordered from the lowest to the highest frequency.** In **Mode 1**, all three masses move together in one broad motion. In **Mode 2**, the middle mass stays still. In **Mode 3**, each mass moves opposite to its neighbor.", "mechanism": "In Mode 2, the two outer masses move in opposite directions, so their pulls on the middle mass balance and it does not move. In Mode 3, the direction switches between every pair of neighbors. So the springs stretch the most, and Mode 3 has the highest frequency.", "consequence": "So extra mass on the middle mass **lowers Mode 1 and Mode 3**, where that mass moves. It **does not change Mode 2**, because the middle mass sits at a **node** (a spot that does not move) in the ideal symmetric system.", "plain": "The middle mass is still in Mode 2, so extra mass there leaves Mode 2 unchanged.", "q1": {"question": "What makes the three normal modes of a three-mass system different?", "answer": "**Each mode is a different pattern with its own frequency.** In Mode 1 all three masses move together. In Mode 2 the middle mass stays still while the outer masses move in opposite directions. In Mode 3 each mass moves opposite to its neighbor, so it has the highest frequency."}, "q2": {"question": "Why does adding mass only to the middle mass leave Mode 2 unchanged?", "answer": "**Mode 2 does not change because the middle mass does not move in Mode 2.** Extra mass only matters where a mode is moving. The middle mass is a node in Mode 2, so its extra inertia has no motion to slow down."}}, "many-modes": {"opening": "**A system with N masses that can each move on their own has N normal modes.** This holds when the masses move in only one direction. Each added mass allows one more pattern of vibration.", "mechanism": "The lower modes have broad patterns, and the higher modes switch direction more often along the system. The system does not have to pick one mode, because it can vibrate in several modes at once.", "consequence": "So with a huge number of tiny masses and springs, the system becomes a **string**. A string has a whole series of modes, and their spacing also depends on its shape and on how its ends are held.", "plain": "Each added mass adds a mode, and endless tiny masses make a string.", "q1": {"question": "Why does a system gain a mode every time you add a mass that can move on its own?", "answer": "**Each new mass adds one more independent way to move, so it adds one more mode.** In the one-direction model, N masses give N modes. So adding a mass always adds a mode."}, "q2": {"question": "What happens when the masses and springs become a continuous string?", "answer": "**The many separate masses turn into a string with a whole series of modes.** The mass and the springiness are spread all along the string. The string still has modes, but their spacing also depends on its shape and on how its ends are held."}}, "fixed-fixed": {"opening": "**A uniform string fixed at both ends must have a node at each end, because neither end can move.** A **node** is a spot that does not move. **Mode 1** fits one half-wave between the ends, and the next modes fit two, three and more half-waves.", "mechanism": "Fitting more half-waves into the same length makes each wave shorter. The wave speed stays the same, so shorter waves have higher frequencies. That is why the modes are **whole-number multiples** of Mode 1, called the **harmonics**.", "consequence": "So if Mode 1 is 200 Hz, **Mode 5 is 1,000 Hz**. A spectrum up to Mode 6 has peaks at 200, 400, 600, 800, 1,000 and 1,200 Hz, with frequency on the horizontal axis.", "plain": "Fixed at both ends gives every whole-number multiple of the first frequency.", "q1": {"question": "How do two fixed ends decide which patterns a string can have?", "answer": "**Only patterns that are still at both ends can fit the string.** Both ends must be nodes, because they cannot move. Whole numbers of half-waves start and end still, so those are the allowed modes."}, "q2": {"question": "Why are the allowed frequencies whole-number multiples of the first one?", "answer": "**More half-waves in the same length make shorter waves, and shorter waves have higher frequencies.** The wave speed stays fixed, so Mode N has N times the frequency of Mode 1. With Mode 1 at 200 Hz, Mode 5 is 1,000 Hz."}}, "membrane": {"opening": "**A membrane is a stretched sheet, so it can vibrate in patterns across a whole surface, not just along a line.** Different parts can move together or in opposite directions. Several of these patterns can be present in one overall motion.", "mechanism": "A measurement at one point picks up every mode that moves at that point. A **spectrum** (a graph of how strong each frequency is) separates those modes by frequency, even when the motion looks messy.", "consequence": "So a spectrum can pull apart a complicated membrane vibration. The evenly spaced frequencies in the membrane demonstration are teaching values, so **not every real membrane has evenly spaced modes**.", "plain": "A membrane vibrates in many surface patterns at once, and a spectrum separates them.", "q1": {"question": "How can several modes be part of one membrane motion?", "answer": "**The modes add together into one overall motion.** The membrane can carry several organized patterns at the same time. The motion you see is the sum of all of them, so it looks complicated."}, "q2": {"question": "What can a measurement at one point and its spectrum show about a mix of membrane modes?", "answer": "**The point records the modes that move there, and the spectrum separates their frequencies.** Each mode shows up as its own peak. A point at a node of one mode does not move in that mode, so it misses that mode."}}, "scientific-method": {"opening": "**A theory can be tested when it makes a prediction you can measure.** The theory that the vocal folds vibrate as a membrane predicts **evenly spaced harmonics** in a recorded voice. **Harmonics** are the peaks in the spectrum of a repeating sound.", "mechanism": "The prediction links the proposed mechanism to a pattern you can see. Recording a voice and checking its spectrum tests whether the peaks have the expected spacing. So you accept the theory because the measurement matches, not because it sounds right.", "consequence": "So a good test connects the theory, the predicted spectrum and the measurement. A matching result supports the prediction, but **it does not prove every part of the theory**.", "plain": "A theory makes a prediction, and a measurement checks it.", "q1": {"question": "How can you test a prediction about voice harmonics with a recording?", "answer": "**Record a voice, look at its spectrum, and measure the distances between the peaks.** The membrane theory predicts evenly spaced harmonics. If the peaks are evenly spaced, the recording supports the prediction."}, "q2": {"question": "How do a theory, a prediction and a measurement fit together?", "answer": "**The theory proposes how something works, the prediction says what pattern to expect, and the measurement checks for it.** A real test needs all three linked together. Without that link, a theory is only an idea that sounds plausible."}}, "fixed-free": {"opening": "**A fixed-free system has one fixed end and one free end, so its two ends behave differently.** The fixed end cannot move, so it is a node. The free end moves the most.", "mechanism": "One **quarter-wave** goes from a still point to a point of greatest motion. To keep the free end moving the most, the next mode adds two more quarter-waves. That gives 1, 3, 5 and 7 quarter-waves.", "consequence": "So the modes are **odd multiples** of the lowest one, at 1, 3, 5 and 7 times. The air in a neutral vocal tract is fixed at the closed glottis and free at the open lips. A building is also fixed at one end and free at the other, at the ground and at the top.", "plain": "Fixed at one end and free at the other gives only odd multiples.", "q1": {"question": "Why do only odd numbers of quarter-waves fit a fixed-free system?", "answer": "**Only an odd number of quarter-waves starts still at the fixed end and ends moving the most at the free end.** Each new mode needs two more quarter-waves to keep the free end moving. An even number would end at a still point, which is wrong for a free end."}, "q2": {"question": "What do a neutral vocal tract and a building have in common?", "answer": "**Both have one fixed end and one free end.** The air in the vocal tract is fixed at the closed glottis and free at the open lips. A building is fixed to the ground and free at the top. The materials and directions of motion differ, but the end conditions match."}}, "economy": {"opening": "**A dynamical model describes how something changes over time, and it can describe an economy as well as a spring.** In an economic model, the variables are economic states, not the position of a block. The model can show a quantity moving back toward an **equilibrium** (a stable level).", "mechanism": "**Feedback** is the effect a change has back on itself. Depending on how the parts connect, feedback can shrink a change or make it grow. So a change in one part can affect the later behavior of the whole system.", "consequence": "So the economy and the spring share **the way change is organized over time**. They do not share the same physical material.", "plain": "An economy and a spring can follow the same rules of change over time.", "q1": {"question": "How can an economic model and a spring share the same kind of feedback?", "answer": "**Both describe a move away from a stable level and rules that change it over time.** In both, feedback can pull the system back or push it further away. What they share is that feedback, not their material."}, "q2": {"question": "What has to match for two very different systems to be compared?", "answer": "**The causes and effects behind the prediction have to match.** Two systems can look alike on the surface and still work differently. A mechanism only carries over when the cause-to-effect links are the same."}}, "local-change": {"opening": "**In a vibrating system, a change at one spot affects each mode according to what that spot does in that mode.** Extra mass matters where the mode moves. Extra stiffness matters where the mode stretches or compresses.", "mechanism": "Where a mode moves the most, the extra mass has the biggest effect, so **the frequency goes down**. At a node, the spot does not move but is stretched the most. So extra stiffness **raises the frequency**, with no mass effect working against it.", "consequence": "So one spot can be a node in one mode and a point of greatest motion in another. Adding mass and stiffness there can raise one mode and lower the other, so **the two changes do not cancel**.", "plain": "Ask whether the spot is moving or stretching in that mode.", "q1": {"question": "Why does the effect of extra mass at one spot depend on the mode?", "answer": "**Extra mass only matters where that mode is moving.** If the spot moves a lot in a mode, the added inertia slows that mode and lowers its frequency. If the spot is still in a mode, the extra mass has no effect on it."}, "q2": {"question": "How can the same added mass and stiffness move two modes in opposite directions?", "answer": "**The spot is mostly stretching in one mode and mostly moving in the other.** In the mode where it stretches, the extra stiffness wins and the frequency goes up. In the mode where it moves, the extra mass wins and the frequency goes down."}}, "air-modes": {"opening": "**The air in the vocal tract moves longitudinally, back and forth along the tube from the glottis to the lips.** The wave-shaped curve in the diagram shows how far the air moves at each spot. The air does not travel up and down inside the mouth.", "mechanism": "Where neighboring bits of air move toward each other, the air is **compressed** and the pressure rises. Where they move apart, the air spreads out. So how much the air moves and how much it is compressed are two different things.", "consequence": "So near a node, the air hardly moves but is **strongly compressed**. Near the spot of greatest motion, the air moves a lot but is hardly compressed. That is why a constriction has different effects in different places.", "plain": "Where the air moves the least, it is compressed the most.", "q1": {"question": "The curve in the vocal tract diagram goes up and down. Does the air move up and down?", "answer": "**No, the air moves back and forth along the tube.** The height of the curve only shows how far the air moves at each spot. The curve is a graph, not the path of the air."}, "q2": {"question": "Why can the air be strongly compressed at a spot where it hardly moves?", "answer": "**Compression depends on how the neighboring air moves, not on how much the spot itself moves.** At a node, the air on both sides moves toward the spot and then away from it. So the air there is crowded and spread out the most, even though it barely moves."}}, "vowel-shape": {"opening": "**Vowels differ in the shape of the vocal tract, the tube from the glottis to the lips.** The shape depends on where the tongue makes a **constriction** (narrowing) and whether the lips are rounded. These changes alter the **resonances** of the air, the frequencies the tube vibrates at most easily.", "mechanism": "A high front tongue makes the vowel in **dean**. A high back tongue with rounded lips makes the vowel in **dune**. A low tongue makes an open vowel, such as **/a/**.", "consequence": "So the location of the constriction decides how the air modes change. The same voice from the vocal folds can pass through different shapes and come out as **different vowels**.", "plain": "Move the tongue and lips, and the resonances and the vowel change.", "q1": {"question": "How do the tongue and lips change which vowel you hear?", "answer": "**They change the location and shape of the air passage, which changes the resonances.** The tongue sets where the constriction is, and the lips can be rounded or not. Different resonances give different vowel qualities."}, "q2": {"question": "Why can one voice source make several different vowels?", "answer": "**The same source can pass through different vocal tract shapes.** The vocal folds are the source, and the vocal tract is the filter. Changing the filter changes the vowel, so no new source is needed for each vowel."}}, "u": {"opening": "**In the /u/ model, a constriction at the lips lowers both F1 and F2.** F1 and F2 are the first two **formants**, the resonances of the vocal tract. The lips are at the open end of the tract, where the first two modes both move a lot.", "mechanism": "At the lips, the constriction adds mass to air that is moving in both modes. More inertia slows both modes, so **F1 and F2 go down together**.", "consequence": "So the result comes from **where the constriction is**, not just from having a constriction. A constriction deeper inside the tract can affect the two modes in different ways.", "plain": "The air at the lips moves in both modes, so both formants go down for /u/.", "q1": {"question": "Why does a constriction at the lips lower both F1 and F2 for /u/?", "answer": "**The air at the lips moves a lot in both Mode 1 and Mode 2.** The constriction adds mass there, so it slows both modes. So both formants go down together."}, "q2": {"question": "Why does the location of a constriction matter more than just calling it a constriction?", "answer": "**The same constriction does different things in different places.** At another spot, the air may be moving in one mode and compressed in the other. So the word constriction alone does not tell you whether mass or stiffness wins."}}, "i": {"opening": "**The /i/ in dean has a low F1 and a high F2.** Its constriction is at the front, about two-thirds of the way from the glottis. That spot plays a different role in each of the first two modes.", "mechanism": "In Mode 1, the air at that spot is mostly moving, so the added mass **lowers F1**. In Mode 2, the air there is mostly compressed, so the added stiffness **raises F2**.", "consequence": "So one change in the shape of the tract moves the two formants in opposite directions. The explanation has to look at each mode separately, not treat the whole air column as one oscillator.", "plain": "For dean, F1 goes down and F2 goes up.", "q1": {"question": "Why does the front constriction for /i/ lower F1 but raise F2?", "answer": "**The constriction adds mass where Mode 1 moves and stiffness where Mode 2 compresses.** In Mode 1 the air there is moving, so the extra mass lowers F1. In Mode 2 the air there is compressed, so the extra stiffness raises F2."}, "q2": {"question": "For /i/, what is the air at the constriction doing in Mode 1 and in Mode 2?", "answer": "**In Mode 1 the air is mostly moving, and in Mode 2 it is mostly compressed.** So mass wins in Mode 1, and F1 goes down. Stiffness wins in Mode 2, and F2 goes up."}}, "a": {"opening": "**The /a/ in Don raises F1 and lowers F2, compared with the neutral tube.** Its constriction is about one-third of the way from the glottis. At that spot, the roles in the first two modes are the reverse of /i/.", "mechanism": "In Mode 1, the air there is mostly compressed, so the added stiffness **raises F1**. In Mode 2, the air there is mostly moving, so the added mass **lowers F2**.", "consequence": "So F1 and F2 end up **closer together** than in /i/. Their pattern follows from where the tract is narrowed.", "plain": "For Don, F1 goes up and F2 goes down.", "q1": {"question": "Why does /a/ raise F1 and lower F2?", "answer": "**At the /a/ constriction, the air is compressed in Mode 1 and moving in Mode 2.** The extra stiffness raises F1, because compression wins in Mode 1. The extra mass lowers F2, because motion wins in Mode 2."}, "q2": {"question": "How does moving the constriction reverse the pattern of /i/?", "answer": "**Moving the constriction changes what the air does there in both modes.** At two-thirds of the way, for /i/, the air moves in Mode 1 and is compressed in Mode 2. At one-third, for /a/, it is the other way around, so the formant changes flip."}}, "constriction": {"opening": "**A constriction (narrowing) in the vocal tract changes both the inertia and the stiffness of the air at that spot.** **Inertia** is the air's resistance to being moved, and **stiffness** is its push back against compression. Which effect matters depends on the mode and the location.", "mechanism": "Where the air is mostly moving in a mode, the added inertia wins and that formant **goes down**. Where the air is mostly compressed, the added stiffness wins and that formant **goes up**. The two effects do not cancel, because motion and compression are strongest in different places.", "consequence": "So a big rise in a formant means the air at the constriction was **strongly compressed and barely moving** in that mode. A big fall means the air there was moving a lot.", "plain": "Moving air means the formant goes down, and compressed air means it goes up.", "q1": {"question": "Why does a constriction change more than one property of the air?", "answer": "**A constriction changes both the inertia of the air and its stiffness.** Narrowing the passage changes how hard the air is to move and how it pushes back when compressed. So both effects have to be weighed within the pattern of each mode."}, "q2": {"question": "A constriction makes a formant rise a lot. What does that tell you about the air there?", "answer": "**The air there was mostly compressed and hardly moving in that mode.** A rise means the stiffness effect won. Stiffness wins where compression is strong and motion is small."}}, "filter": {"opening": "**In the source-filter model, the vocal tract is the filter, and it changes how strong each harmonic is.** It strengthens frequencies near its resonances, which are called **formants**. **F1** and **F2** are the first two formants.", "mechanism": "Harmonics close to a formant stay strong, and harmonics far from any formant get weaker. The outline of the harmonic heights is the **envelope**, and its broad peaks show the shape of the tract.", "consequence": "So changing the vowel can move the formants while **F0** (the fundamental frequency of the voice) stays the same. Changing F0 changes the spacing of the harmonics while the tract shape stays about the same. So **pitch and vowel are separate**.", "plain": "The source makes the harmonics, and the filter decides which ones are strong.", "q1": {"question": "How do formants decide which harmonics are strong?", "answer": "**The tract strengthens harmonics near its formants and weakens those farther away.** So the harmonics near a formant stand out as tall peaks. The envelope over the harmonic heights carries the formant pattern."}, "q2": {"question": "Why are pitch and vowel quality two different things?", "answer": "**Pitch comes from the source, and vowel quality comes from the filter.** Pitch depends on how fast the vocal folds repeat, which is F0. Vowel quality depends on the formants of the vocal tract, so each one can change without the other."}}, "spectrogram": {"opening": "**A spectrum shows the frequencies in a sound at one moment, and a spectrogram shows them changing over time.** In a spectrogram, time runs along the horizontal axis. Frequency runs up the vertical axis.", "mechanism": "Darkness shows **amplitude** (strength), so darker areas have stronger energy at that time and frequency. The broad dark **formant bands** rise and fall as the vocal tract changes shape.", "consequence": "So going from /i/ to /a/, **the F1 band rises and the F2 band falls**. Reading the direction of the bands is enough, without tracing every value.", "plain": "Time goes across, frequency goes up, and darkness shows strength.", "q1": {"question": "What can a spectrogram show that one spectrum cannot?", "answer": "**A spectrogram shows how the frequencies change over time.** It lines up the spectra of one short stretch of time after another. A single spectrum covers only one moment, so it loses that change."}, "q2": {"question": "What do the horizontal axis, the vertical axis and the darkness show in a spectrogram?", "answer": "**Time is horizontal, frequency is vertical, and darkness is amplitude.** Each one has to be read separately. The darkest broad bands are the formants."}}, "sparse-harmonics": {"opening": "**A high F0 spreads the harmonics far apart, so fewer of them fall near each formant.** A formant can then sit between two harmonics. No nearby peaks are left to show its shape.", "mechanism": "A low voice has many closely spaced harmonics around each formant, so the envelope is easy to see. A high voice has only a few, so the envelope is **hard to estimate** from the spectrum.", "consequence": "So vowels can be hard to identify in very high singing or high-pitched speech. **The tract still has its formants**, and a formant does not turn into the fundamental.", "plain": "High F0 leaves few harmonics near each formant, so the vowel is harder to read.", "q1": {"question": "Why can widely spaced harmonics hide a vowel's formants?", "answer": "**Big gaps leave only a few harmonics near each formant.** The envelope is traced from the harmonic peaks. With so few peaks, the envelope and its formants are hard to see."}, "q2": {"question": "If a formant is hard to see, does that mean it is gone?", "answer": "**No, the vocal tract still has the formant.** It is hard to see because no harmonic falls close to its peak. Having too few harmonics near a formant is not the same as having no formant."}}, "source": {"opening": "**In the source-filter model, the vocal folds are the source, and they make a harmonic series.** The lowest harmonic is the **fundamental frequency**, F0. The other harmonics fall at whole-number multiples of F0.", "mechanism": "A faster repeating source spaces the harmonics farther apart. Stiffer vocal folds tend to **raise F0**, and more vibrating mass in the folds tends to **lower F0**.", "consequence": "So at an F0 of 150 Hz, the first five harmonics are 150, 300, 450, 600 and 750 Hz. These numbers describe the source, and the tract decides how strong each one is.", "plain": "F0 sets the spacing of the harmonics.", "q1": {"question": "What decides how far apart the harmonics are?", "answer": "**F0 decides the spacing.** The harmonics fall at whole-number multiples of F0. So with an F0 of 150 Hz, the harmonics are 150 Hz apart."}, "q2": {"question": "Why do the mass and stiffness of the vocal folds change F0?", "answer": "**Stiffer folds repeat faster, and heavier folds repeat more slowly.** More stiffness gives a stronger pull back, so F0 rises. More vibrating mass has more inertia, so F0 falls."}}, "continuous-speech": {"opening": "**In connected speech, the formants keep changing, because the tongue and lips keep moving between sounds.** In the phrase \"Where were you a year ago?\", the sound never settles into a row of steady vowels.", "mechanism": "F2 rises when the tongue moves toward the shape for /i/. Lip rounding lowers the formants toward the pattern for /u/. So the moving bands link the movements of the mouth to the sound over time.", "consequence": "So **the direction of the bands** across a stretch of speech carries information. A sound can be identified from its transitions, not only from a slice in its middle.", "plain": "The mouth keeps moving in speech, and the formant bands move with it.", "q1": {"question": "Why do the formant bands keep moving in connected speech?", "answer": "**The vocal tract keeps changing shape as speech goes on.** Each new shape has new resonances. So the formant bands rise and fall instead of staying flat."}, "q2": {"question": "What do the formant transitions add beyond the middle of a vowel?", "answer": "**The transitions carry information about how the mouth is moving and about the neighboring sounds.** A slice from the steady middle of a vowel leaves that information out."}}, "splice": {"opening": "**A consonant is identified partly from the vowel that follows it.** When the consonant from ma is joined to the vowel from na, listeners can hear na. The vowel from na carries a **formant transition** (a change in the formants) left by the /n/.", "mechanism": "The vowel after a nasal keeps information about the mouth movement before it. When that transition conflicts with the consonant, the information in the vowel can win and decide what listeners hear.", "consequence": "So **speech recognition uses information spread over time**, and listeners do not decide each tiny slice on its own. This result does not mean that only large language models can recognize speech.", "plain": "The vowel carries information about the consonant before it.", "q1": {"question": "Why can the vowel from na change what you hear for the consonant from ma?", "answer": "**The vowel from na still carries the transition from the /n/.** Listeners use that information from the vowel. When it conflicts with the consonant, the vowel can win, so listeners hear na."}, "q2": {"question": "What does the splicing result mean for identifying speech one slice at a time?", "answer": "**You cannot identify a sound from its own tiny slice alone.** What listeners hear depends on information that spreads across neighboring sounds. Classifying each slice on its own leaves that context out."}}, "vowel-network": {"opening": "**The vowel network takes F1 and F2 as its two inputs and gives a probability for each of five words.** The five outputs stand for done, dune, dean, Don and Dan. Each output position belongs to one word.", "mechanism": "The hidden layers turn the two formants into new numbers that the last layer uses. The learned **weights** decide which formant patterns support which word.", "consequence": "So a low F1 with a high F2 matches /i/, and **the right answer for that input is dean**. Getting it right depends on learning how formants relate to words, not on memorizing the order of the recordings.", "plain": "Two formants go in, and five word probabilities come out.", "q1": {"question": "How do F1 and F2 work as inputs to the vowel network?", "answer": "**F1 and F2 are the two measured numbers fed into the network.** The weights turn them into support for each of the five words. Each output position stands for one possible vowel."}, "q2": {"question": "Why does a low F1 and a high F2 go with dean?", "answer": "**A low F1 and a high F2 is the pattern for /i/, the vowel in dean.** So dean is the correct target for that input. That stays true even if an untrained network currently picks a different word."}}, "network-shape": {"opening": "**The vowel network has 2 inputs, three hidden layers and 5 output neurons.** The hidden layers have 3, 2 and 4 neurons, in that order. The 2 inputs are F1 and F2, and the 5 outputs are the five words.", "mechanism": "Every layer is **fully connected**, so each neuron gets one weight from every neuron in the layer before it. So the number of weights into one neuron depends on the size of the layer before, not on the number of outputs.", "consequence": "So the input layer only supplies F1 and F2 and does no processing. That leaves **four processing layers**, which match the four Linear lines in the program.", "plain": "Hidden layers of 3, 2 and 4 neurons lead to 5 outputs.", "q1": {"question": "How many hidden layers does the vowel network have?", "answer": "**It has three hidden layers, with 3, 2 and 4 neurons.** The layer of 5 neurons at the end is the output layer, not a hidden layer. The 2 inputs only supply F1 and F2."}, "q2": {"question": "What decides how many weights go into one neuron?", "answer": "**The size of the layer before it decides the number.** In a fully connected layer, each incoming value has its own weight. So a neuron after a layer of 4 neurons has 4 incoming weights."}}, "weights": {"opening": "**In a neural network, a weight controls how much an incoming activation adds to the next neuron.** The receiving neuron multiplies each input by its weight. Then it adds up the results, which gives the **weighted sum**, also called the **inner product**.", "mechanism": "A positive weight keeps the sign of the input, and a negative weight flips it. The size of the weight sets how strong the contribution is, so **a large weight is not automatically a good one**.", "consequence": "So random starting weights have not learned the vowels yet. Training improves the network by changing the weights in response to its errors.", "plain": "Multiply each input by its weight, then add the results.", "q1": {"question": "How does a neuron combine its weighted inputs?", "answer": "**It multiplies each input by its weight and adds the results.** That sum is the weighted sum, also called the inner product. The result is one number."}, "q2": {"question": "Why does the sign of a contribution depend on both the input and the weight?", "answer": "**Both signs together decide the direction of the contribution.** A positive weight keeps the sign of the input, and a negative weight flips it. So a positive input with a negative weight pushes the neuron down."}}, "forward": {"opening": "**Forward propagation is how a network makes a prediction with its current weights.** Each processing layer combines the activations from the layer before. Its results become the inputs to the next layer.", "mechanism": "The input layer only supplies the measured values, so it has no weights of its own. The output layer turns the final numbers into the network's decision among the possible words.", "consequence": "So a network can finish a forward pass correctly and **still make the wrong decision**. Making a prediction and improving the weights are separate jobs, but the prediction is needed to find the error.", "plain": "Forward propagation carries the input to an output with the weights the network has now.", "q1": {"question": "What happens during forward propagation?", "answer": "**The input passes through one processing layer after another, using the current weights.** Each layer works on the results of the layer before it. The result at the end is the network's prediction."}, "q2": {"question": "Why can a correct forward pass still give a wrong answer?", "answer": "**The weights may be untrained or poorly learned.** The calculation can be done perfectly with bad weights. So a correct computation can still lead to a wrong decision."}}, "sequential": {"opening": "**In PyTorch, nn.Sequential lists the processing layers in the order their outputs pass along.** In nn.Linear(2, 3), 2 values come in and the layer has 3 neurons. Each layer hands its output to the next one.", "mechanism": "Each layer must take in as many values as the layer before it gives out. So the lines Linear(2, 3), Linear(3, 2), Linear(2, 4) and Linear(4, 5) give **three hidden layers** and an output layer of 5.", "consequence": "So to add a 3-neuron hidden layer before the output, change Linear(4, 5) to Linear(4, 3). Then add Linear(3, 5), so **the sizes still match** from line to line.", "plain": "Each line's first number must match the second number on the line before.", "q1": {"question": "What does nn.Sequential say about the path through a network?", "answer": "**It says the layers run in order, each passing its output to the next.** Because of that order, neighboring layers must agree in size. The second number of one line must match the first number of the next."}, "q2": {"question": "Why must the layer sizes match when you add a hidden layer?", "answer": "**The new layer has to accept what the layer before gives and produce what the next layer expects.** If the sizes do not match, the values cannot pass through. Matching sizes keeps a valid path from input to output."}}, "probabilities": {"opening": "**A probability distribution is a list of numbers that are never negative and add up to 1.** The output of the vowel network is one such list. Each number is the chance the network gives to one word.", "mechanism": "The position of each number tells you which word it belongs to. The **target** (desired output) for dean puts 1 in the dean position and 0 everywhere else. That gives training a specific answer to aim for.", "consequence": "So **a valid distribution can still favor the wrong word**. Validity checks the form of the output, and accuracy checks whether the favored word matches the recording.", "plain": "Valid means it adds up to 1, and correct means it picks the right word.", "q1": {"question": "What makes a list of numbers a valid probability distribution?", "answer": "**Every number must be zero or more, and all of them must add up to 1.** A list does not count just because a network produced it. It has to pass both checks across all the words."}, "q2": {"question": "How is the correct target different from a prediction that is valid but wrong?", "answer": "**The target marks the known word, but a valid prediction can favor a different word.** The target for dean has a 1 for dean and 0 for the rest. A wrong prediction can still add up to 1, so validity and accuracy are separate tests."}}, "weight-direction": {"opening": "**To decide whether changing a weight helps, trace it through the sign of the incoming activation and what the output needs.** With a positive incoming activation, raising a weight into an output raises that output's **raw score** (its number before it becomes a probability).", "mechanism": "If that word already gets too much probability, raising its score works against the target. For an early hidden weight, the effect also depends on all the weights and paths after it.", "consequence": "So a good answer states the local effect and links it to the error. It does not assume that **raising every weight helps**, or that all hidden weights push the decision the same way.", "plain": "Check the incoming sign and whether that output should go up or down.", "q1": {"question": "How do you tell whether raising an output weight helps lower the error?", "answer": "**Raising it helps only if it moves the output the way the target needs.** Check the sign of the incoming activation to see which way the output score moves. Then check whether the target needs that output to go up or down."}, "q2": {"question": "Why is the effect of an early hidden weight harder to trace?", "answer": "**Its effect passes through every later activation and weight before it reaches the output.** So the final effect depends on all those later paths. Its own sign alone is not enough to tell."}}, "backward": {"opening": "**Backpropagation is how a network works out how each weight should change, and it starts at the output.** The error is most directly tied to the last processing layer. An early weight affects the result only through several later layers, so its effect is harder to work out directly.", "mechanism": "The algorithm works out the error information for the later layers first. Then it reuses that information to find the effects of earlier weights. Solving the smaller dependent problems first and reusing them is **dynamic programming**.", "consequence": "So backpropagation computes the **gradients**, which say how the error changes when each weight changes. Then an optimizer such as **SGD** (stochastic gradient descent) uses the gradients to change the weights. The backward step uses the values from the forward pass.", "plain": "Start at the output, reuse each answer, and move backward.", "q1": {"question": "Why does backpropagation work out the later layers before the earlier ones?", "answer": "**The effects of earlier weights depend on the later layers.** Once the gradients for the later layers are known, they can be reused for the earlier ones. That reuse is dynamic programming."}, "q2": {"question": "What do dynamic programming and SGD each do in learning?", "answer": "**Dynamic programming reuses calculations to find the gradients, and SGD uses the gradients to change the weights.** Working out the gradients is one step. Updating the weights is a separate step."}}, "training-data": {"opening": "**In the training data, each input row must stay paired with its correct target row.** The input row holds one recording's F1 and F2. The target row says which of the five words that recording is.", "mechanism": "For dean, the target row has a 1 in the dean column and 0 in the others. If an input row moved without its target, the network would **learn the wrong pairing**, even though every number still looks fine.", "consequence": "So the input table has two columns, F1 and F2, and the target table has five columns, one per word. The small example tables show this layout, not the size of the full dataset.", "plain": "Each recording's formants must stay with its vowel.", "q1": {"question": "Why must each row of formants stay paired with its vowel?", "answer": "**The two rows describe the same recording, its measurements and its known vowel.** The network learns by comparing its guess with that vowel. So the rows must line up for the training to be correct."}, "q2": {"question": "What would a mismatch between rows teach the network?", "answer": "**It would teach the network the wrong link between formants and words.** One recording's formants would be paired with a different vowel. Each update would then push the weights toward that wrong pairing."}}, "training-code": {"opening": "**The training code splits learning into four jobs, which are predicting, computing the loss, computing the gradients and updating the weights.** Importing **PyTorch** (torch) brings in ready-made programs for these jobs. So none of these algorithms has to be written again by hand.", "mechanism": "The **loss** compares the prediction with the targets to measure the mistake. Then loss.backward() runs backpropagation to compute the gradients. The optimizer's step updates the weights using those gradients and the **learning rate** (the size of each step).", "consequence": "So the old gradients are cleared before each step, because otherwise they would pile up. Knowing **what each line does** matters more than memorizing every dot, bracket or number.", "plain": "Predict, measure the loss, run backward, take a step, and repeat.", "q1": {"question": "What do the loss, backpropagation and the optimizer step each do?", "answer": "**The loss measures the mistake, backpropagation computes the gradients, and the step changes the weights.** These are three separate stages of learning. Each stage uses the result of the one before it."}, "q2": {"question": "Why are the gradients cleared before the next training step?", "answer": "**Stored gradients would otherwise pile up across steps.** Clearing them lets each step compute fresh gradients for the current prediction. It does not reset the weights the network has already learned."}}, "sigmoid": {"opening": "**The sigmoid turns a neuron's weighted sum into a value between 0 and 1.** A large negative sum gives a value near 0, and a large positive sum gives a value near 1. A sum of exactly 0 gives 0.5.", "mechanism": "The S-shaped curve is steep in the middle and flat near both ends. So the same change in the input has a big effect near the middle and a small effect near the ends.", "consequence": "So adding the sigmoid between linear layers makes the network **nonlinear** (no longer just weighted sums). Each hidden value is between 0 and 1, but the values need not add up to 1, so **they are not a probability distribution**.", "plain": "A large negative sum goes to about 0, zero goes to 0.5, and a large positive sum goes to about 1.", "q1": {"question": "What does the sigmoid do to a negative, zero or positive sum?", "answer": "**A large negative sum comes out near 0, a sum of 0 comes out as 0.5, and a large positive sum comes out near 1.** The output always stays between 0 and 1. The curve is S-shaped, so the response is nonlinear."}, "q2": {"question": "Is a hidden neuron's sigmoid value a probability for a vowel?", "answer": "**No, it is just a value between 0 and 1.** Each hidden value is limited on its own, but their total can be anything. A probability distribution must also add up to 1."}}, "sigmoid-flow": {"opening": "**When the sigmoid changes a hidden value, every later layer changes too.** The later layers take that hidden value as their input. So the rest of the network has to be recalculated, not just one number replaced.", "mechanism": "Each hidden neuron first adds up its weighted inputs, and then it applies the sigmoid. The output of the sigmoid is the value that travels along the outgoing connections.", "consequence": "So **the final word probabilities can change** even though F1 and F2 stayed the same. Adding the sigmoid changes the processing between the measurements and the decision.", "plain": "Change a hidden value, and everything after it changes.", "q1": {"question": "Why do later values change when the sigmoid transforms a hidden activation?", "answer": "**The later layers use the transformed value as their input.** If that input changes, their weighted sums change too. So their activations and the final decision must be recalculated."}, "q2": {"question": "How does putting a sigmoid between layers change what the network does?", "answer": "**It adds a nonlinear step between the weighted sums.** That changes the function the network computes. The change happens even before any weight is updated."}}, "overlap": {"opening": "**When the vowel recordings are plotted by F1 and F2, the vowel categories overlap.** Each point is one recording, placed by its two formant values. So one pair of formants does not always point to just one vowel.", "mechanism": "Nearby points have similar formants even when they are different vowels. So the network must learn useful boundaries from the labeled examples. It cannot expect each vowel to have its own separate region.", "consequence": "So the plot shows how hard the problem is before training is judged. **Accuracy on the training data** does not by itself show how well the network does on new speakers.", "plain": "The vowels overlap, so some guesses will be wrong.", "q1": {"question": "What does the overlap between vowels in the F1 and F2 plot mean for recognition?", "answer": "**Different vowels can have nearly the same F1 and F2.** So those two measurements do not split the vowels into fully separate regions. For points in the overlap, some decisions will be wrong."}, "q2": {"question": "Why does training accuracy only tell you about the training examples?", "answer": "**Training accuracy counts correct decisions on those particular recordings.** It says nothing direct about recordings the network has never seen. So testing on new speakers needs a separate check."}}, "accuracy": {"opening": "**Accuracy is the share of decisions the network gets right.** With five equally likely words, random guessing is right one time in five. So **chance accuracy is 20 percent**.", "mechanism": "Accuracy can rise as the weights learn useful differences, though the curve may jump around before it settles. When it levels off, more updates in that run are no longer improving the training decisions.", "consequence": "So this linear network's run **levels off near 45 percent** on its training data. That result describes this network and this run, not a limit for all neural networks or vowel classifiers.", "plain": "Chance is 20 percent, and this run levels off near 45 percent.", "q1": {"question": "Why is one in five a useful starting point for five equally likely words?", "answer": "**Random guessing among five equally likely words is right 20 percent of the time.** Only one of the five choices is correct. So 20 percent is the level a network must beat to show it has learned something."}, "q2": {"question": "What does it mean when training accuracy levels off?", "answer": "**The share of correct decisions has stopped rising in that run.** So more training is no longer helping. Leveling off below 100 percent means the network still makes mistakes."}}, "initialization": {"opening": "**Different starting weights can lead to different learning paths on the same data.** The starting weights are random. They change the early predictions and gradients, even when the network and the training steps stay the same.", "mechanism": "The ten runs shown take different routes but reach **the same final training accuracy**. So the path can vary while the end result stays the same.", "consequence": "So repeated runs give stronger evidence about this training setup than one chosen run. Their agreement does not promise the exact same number with every software library or every starting point.", "plain": "Different paths lead to the same end point.", "q1": {"question": "How can different starting weights give different learning paths?", "answer": "**Different starting weights make different early predictions, so the corrections differ too.** Each correction depends on the error before it. So the path of learning changes, even on the same task."}, "q2": {"question": "What does it mean that the runs end at the same accuracy?", "answer": "**The end point is the same even though the paths differ.** The ten runs took different routes but reached the same training accuracy. Agreeing at the end is not the same as following the same path."}}, "brain-model": {"opening": "**A model neuron is a math version of a real neuron that links incoming activity, connection strengths and one output.** It simplifies what a real neuron does into steps that can be calculated and studied.", "mechanism": "The weights stand for how strongly each incoming signal counts. The weighted sum captures one part of how a neuron gathers information. It leaves out the chemical and electrical details of a real neuron.", "consequence": "So the model is useful for the behavior it helps explain. Borrowing the idea from biology **does not make a network the same as a brain**.", "plain": "A model neuron keeps the weighted adding up and drops the biological details.", "q1": {"question": "What does a model neuron simplify about a real neuron?", "answer": "**It keeps the links between incoming activity, connection strengths and the response, and drops the biological details.** The weighted sum stands for how the neuron gathers its inputs. The chemistry and electricity of a real neuron are left out."}, "q2": {"question": "Why should a model inspired by the brain not be treated as a copy of the brain?", "answer": "**A model can be useful without copying every physical detail.** It only keeps one part of what a real neuron does. So the model needs its own evidence that it works."}}, "recurrent": {"opening": "**A recurrent network sends its own output back into part of its input, so each step depends on the one before.** A regular network classifies one static vector at a time. A recurrent network carries information from one moment to the next.", "mechanism": "Michael Jordan invented the recurrent network in 1985 to model **speech production**, how the tongue, velum and lips move during speech. Speech production and prediction both depend on information that unfolds over time. The feedback gives the next step its context.", "consequence": "So the network keeps transforming its information while holding on to **the context it needs**. GPT also feeds each new word back in, though modern language models add other mechanisms besides recurrence.", "plain": "The output goes back in, so earlier steps shape later ones.", "q1": {"question": "How does a recurrent network let earlier items matter?", "answer": "**It carries its state from one step to the next by feeding its output back in.** What it produced at one moment becomes part of its input at the next. So its current activity depends on earlier context."}, "q2": {"question": "Why does a sequence need context and not just one static input?", "answer": "**What an item is, or what comes after it, can depend on the items before it.** Processing each item alone throws away those relationships. So a network for sequences has to carry context forward."}}, "levels": {"opening": "**AI systems are built in levels, from a neuron to a network to an LLM to an agentic system.** A neuron combines its inputs into one number. A network combines layers of neurons, and an LLM uses context to predict what comes next.", "mechanism": "An **agentic system** connects LLMs through messages and assigned tasks. So one LLM's output can become another LLM's input. These connections sit outside the hidden layers of any single model.", "consequence": "So the same weighted sums stay useful as the system grows more complex. **Adding a higher level does not make the lower-level computations disappear.**", "plain": "Neurons make networks, networks make LLMs, and LLMs make agentic systems.", "q1": {"question": "How are a neuron, a network and an agentic system different levels?", "answer": "**Each level is built from the one below it.** A neuron combines inputs, and a network combines processing layers. An agentic system connects whole LLMs that each have a task."}, "q2": {"question": "Why do the lower-level computations still matter in a large agentic system?", "answer": "**The higher levels are built out of the lower computations.** An agentic system is made of LLMs. Each LLM still runs on the weighted sums inside its layers."}}, "next-token": {"opening": "**An LLM is given a sequence of vectors and predicts the next one, whatever the vectors stand for.** A **vector** is a list of numbers. Words, sounds, images and other data can all be turned into vectors. For example, the model can be given 15 vectors and asked to predict the 16th.", "mechanism": "The model uses the context so far to give a probability to each possible next item. When the real next item arrives, it gives feedback, so learning improves those probabilities.", "consequence": "So the position and context of each item help decide what should come next. **The task stays the same** whether the vectors are words, sounds or amino acids.", "plain": "Given the vectors so far, predict the next one.", "q1": {"question": "What stays the same when an LLM predicts words, sounds or images?", "answer": "**The task stays the same, which is predicting the next vector from the ones before.** Every kind of item is turned into an ordered list of vectors. The material can differ without changing the task."}, "q2": {"question": "How do the items so far shape the probabilities for the next one?", "answer": "**The context changes which next items fit and how likely each one is.** When many items can follow, the probability is spread out. When few can follow, it piles onto those few."}}, "phonotactics": {"opening": "**Phonotactics is the set of rules for which sound sequences a language allows.** English allows some consonant clusters before or after a vowel. It rules out others in the same position, so flat is a word but lfat is not.", "mechanism": "An **onset** comes before the vowel of a syllable, as in split. A **coda** comes after it, as in lamp. A cluster that works across a syllable break may not work at the start of a syllable. So Atlantic is fine, because t and l sit in different syllables, but tlantic is impossible.", "consequence": "So **the rules are about sounds, not letters**. Knee is spelled with a k, but its normal pronunciation does not begin with /k/.", "plain": "Where a cluster sits in the syllable decides whether English allows it.", "q1": {"question": "How do the onset and the coda limit which English sound sequences are possible?", "answer": "**Each position in the syllable allows only certain combinations.** The onset comes before the vowel, and the coda comes after it. So whether a sequence is allowed depends on where it sits in the syllable."}, "q2": {"question": "Why is Atlantic a word but tlantic impossible?", "answer": "**In Atlantic, the t ends one syllable and the l starts the next.** In tlantic, tl would have to start a syllable as one onset. English does not allow tl as an onset, so tlantic is impossible."}}, "letter-guess": {"opening": "**Guessing the next letter of a word shows what you know about English word patterns.** After sp at the start of a word, l, r or a vowel can come next. The letter n cannot come next, because it does not fit the English onset pattern.", "mechanism": "Some beginnings allow many next letters, and others allow only a few. A probability distribution shows this by **spreading out** after ra and **piling up** on a few vowels after skr.", "consequence": "So a network that learns from many completions can pick up these patterns **without being told each rule**. The spelling game tests real language knowledge, but it is not a full description of English sounds.", "plain": "After some beginnings many letters fit, and after others only a few do.", "q1": {"question": "What can guessing the next letter show about word beginnings?", "answer": "**It shows which continuations English allows at the start of a word.** A beginning limits what can come next. After sp, only l, r or a vowel fits, so a good guess reflects those learned limits."}, "q2": {"question": "Why is the next-letter distribution spread out after some beginnings and peaked after others?", "answer": "**Some beginnings allow many next letters, and others allow very few.** After ra, almost any letter can follow, so the distribution is flat. After skr, only a vowel can follow, so it is sharply peaked."}}, "heads": {"opening": "**An LLM is built from layers, and each layer contains several attention heads working side by side.** A **head** is a set of weights that handles one kind of relationship in the input. Layers go one after another from the input to the output.", "mechanism": "Each layer combines what its heads found into improved vectors and passes them on. The next layer then works on vectors that already include those relationships. So heads give the model width, and layers give it depth.", "consequence": "So a head is a part inside one LLM, **not a separate person or agent**. Calling a head an expert does not promise it has a skill people can name. Several heads in one layer are not the same as several whole agents sending messages.", "plain": "Heads sit side by side inside a layer, and layers go one after another.", "q1": {"question": "How are heads different from layers?", "answer": "**Heads work side by side inside one layer, and layers work one after another.** Each layer combines the results of its heads into improved vectors. The next layer then improves those vectors again."}, "q2": {"question": "Why is a head not the same as an agent with a checking job?", "answer": "**A head is a part inside one LLM, but an agent is a whole LLM with a task in a larger system.** A critic agent, for example, checks a draft. So heads and agents belong to different levels."}}, "dynamic-depth": {"opening": "**A dynamical system takes a state and changes it again and again by a rule.** An oscillator updates its position and velocity over time. A layered LLM updates its vectors layer by layer through its depth.", "mechanism": "Following one value through the layers shows how the vectors change. Each layer has its own learned weights, so the comparison is about **repeated change**, not the same rule at every layer.", "consequence": "So in both systems, **the output of one step becomes the input to the next**. The comparison does not mean an LLM layer is a physical spring.", "plain": "In both, one step feeds the next.", "q1": {"question": "What does the depth of an LLM share with a dynamical system changing over time?", "answer": "**In both, the output of one step becomes the input to the next.** The state of the oscillator changes from moment to moment. The vectors of the LLM change from layer to layer."}, "q2": {"question": "Does the dynamical-system comparison mean every layer has the same weights?", "answer": "**No, each layer has its own learned weights.** The comparison is about a series of changing states. It does not need one identical rule at every layer."}}, "software": {"opening": "**Software 1.0, 2.0 and 3.0 are three ways to build a program.** In Software 1.0, a person writes the steps. In Software 2.0, a network learns its weights from examples. In Software 3.0, an agentic system of LLMs builds the program from plain English instructions.", "mechanism": "A Software 1.0 program spells out each operation in a language such as Python. A trained network stores what it learned about inputs and outputs in its weights. In Software 3.0, LLMs can draft, check and revise the result, and doing this from plain English is called **vibe coding**.", "consequence": "So the three **can work together in one system** instead of replacing each other. The agents still rely on trained weights and on programs that run.", "plain": "Write the steps, train the weights, or describe the task in English.", "q1": {"question": "What is the difference between Software 1.0, 2.0 and 3.0?", "answer": "**They differ in how the program gets its behavior.** In 1.0, a person writes the steps. In 2.0, a network learns its weights from examples. In 3.0, communicating LLMs build the program from plain English instructions."}, "q2": {"question": "Why can Software 1.0, 2.0 and 3.0 work together?", "answer": "**An agentic system uses trained networks, and those networks run as written programs.** So all three kinds of software can be part of one system. A newer kind does not have to replace an older one."}}}, "connectionQs": {"modern-connection-oscillation": {"question": "Why does a moving mass keep going past equilibrium even though the spring pulls it back?", "answer": "**Inertia keeps the mass moving when it reaches equilibrium.** Equilibrium is the resting position, but the mass arrives there still moving. The restoring force then works against the motion until the mass turns around. So the opposite half of the cycle begins."}, "modern-connection-modes": {"question": "Why does adding a third mass give the system another normal mode?", "answer": "**Each mass that moves on its own adds one more way for the system to move together.** In the one-direction model, three masses have three modes. Each mode is a pattern of the whole system."}, "modern-connection-perturbation": {"question": "Why can adding mass and stiffness at one spot raise one mode and lower another?", "answer": "**The same spot can be strongly stretched in one mode and strongly moving in another.** Extra stiffness matters where stretching is strong, so that mode goes up. Extra mass matters where motion is strong, so that mode goes down."}, "modern-connection-boundaries": {"question": "Why does a string fixed at both ends have all whole-number harmonics, while a fixed-free string has only odd ones?", "answer": "**The end conditions decide which waves fit.** A string fixed at both ends is still at both ends, so any whole number of half-waves fits. A fixed-free string must go from a still end to an end that moves the most. So only odd numbers of quarter-waves fit."}, "modern-connection-test-theory": {"question": "What can a spectrum let you test about the vibration of the vocal folds?", "answer": "**It lets you check for evenly spaced harmonic peaks.** Evenly spaced peaks are what a repeating source predicts. So the measurement supports the prediction, and the theory is no longer just an untested description."}, "modern-connection-air": {"question": "Can air be strongly compressed at a spot where it hardly moves?", "answer": "**Yes, air can be strongly compressed at a node.** The air on both sides moves toward the node and then away from it. So the density and pressure there change strongly, even though the air at that spot barely moves."}, "modern-connection-vowels": {"question": "What makes /i/ and /a/ different, without memorizing a table of frequencies?", "answer": "**Their constrictions sit at different spots in the mode patterns.** For /i/, F1 goes down and F2 goes up. For /a/, the effects reverse, because compression and motion win in the opposite modes."}, "modern-connection-source-filter": {"question": "Does raising your pitch turn /i/ into /a/?", "answer": "**No, raising pitch changes F0 and the spacing of the harmonics.** The vowel depends mostly on the formant pattern of the vocal tract. Very widely spaced harmonics can make that pattern harder to see, but they do not change the vowel."}, "modern-connection-acoustic-displays": {"question": "How does a spectrogram show a vowel changing?", "answer": "**Time runs across, frequency runs up, and darkness shows amplitude.** The dark formant bands move as the vocal tract changes shape. So following the bands over time shows how the resonances change."}, "modern-connection-context": {"question": "Why can a vowel taken from na change what you hear for a consonant taken from ma?", "answer": "**The vowel from na carries a transition left by the /n/ before it.** Listeners use information across a stretch of speech. So that transition can outweigh where the consonant actually came from."}, "modern-connection-classifier": {"question": "What does a network learn when it takes F1 and F2 as inputs?", "answer": "**The network learns its weights.** The weights decide how the formants shape each hidden layer. In the end, they decide the probabilities for the five vowel words."}, "modern-connection-target": {"question": "Can a prediction add up to 1 and still be wrong?", "answer": "**Yes, adding up to 1 only makes it a valid probability distribution.** It can still favor the wrong vowel. The target marks the actual vowel, so comparing with it gives the error."}, "modern-connection-backward": {"question": "Why does backpropagation start at the output instead of the first hidden layer?", "answer": "**The last layer is most directly connected to the error.** Earlier weights affect the error through more layers in between. So backpropagation works out the later layers first and reuses those results for the earlier ones."}, "modern-connection-program": {"question": "What changes when a 3-neuron hidden layer is added right before the 5 output neurons?", "answer": "**The line before the new layer must now give 3 values, and a new line must take those 3 and give 5.** The input stays at 2, for F1 and F2. All neighboring layer sizes still have to match."}, "modern-connection-nonlinearity": {"question": "Why must the later activations be recalculated after the sigmoid changes a hidden value?", "answer": "**The later layers take the changed value as their input.** So the sigmoid changes everything computed after it. The old final probabilities cannot just be kept."}, "modern-connection-training-result": {"question": "What does it mean when runs with different starting weights reach the same training accuracy?", "answer": "**It supports the same end result for this setup, even though the learning paths differ.** That accuracy is measured on the training examples. So it does not by itself show how well the network does on new speakers."}, "modern-connection-sequence": {"question": "How is predicting the next item different from classifying one vector on its own?", "answer": "**Predicting the next item uses the relationships across the whole sequence so far.** The context from other items can change which next item is most likely. That holds even when one item looks exactly the same."}, "modern-connection-phonotactics": {"question": "Why does a good next-letter guesser show knowledge of English word patterns?", "answer": "**Good guesses have to fit what English allows next.** The rules for sounds and spelling narrow the choices. So learning to complete words well picks up those patterns."}, "modern-connection-architecture": {"question": "How is a critic agent different from an attention head?", "answer": "**An attention head is one computation inside a layer of one model.** A critic agent is a whole model with the job of checking work in a larger system. Its message can guide another agent's revision."}}, "cards": {"modern-card-mass-stiffness": {"title": "What happens when an oscillator gets heavier or stiffer?", "explain": "A mass-spring system vibrates because the spring pulls the mass back toward rest. In this comparison, one copy gets more **mass**, another gets more **stiffness**, and a third stays the same for reference. Counting cycles over the same stretch of time shows the **frequency** (cycles per second). This count is separate from how far the mass moves in each cycle.", "outcome": "**The heavier system completes fewer cycles, and the stiffer system completes more.** Extra mass adds **inertia** (resistance to being moved), so the mass turns around more slowly. A stiffer spring pulls back harder, so the mass turns around more quickly.", "interpret": "A good prediction says what changed and what stayed the same. Making the mass move farther changes the **amplitude** (the size of the movement). Amplitude is a different measure, so it does not answer the frequency question."}, "modern-card-two-masses": {"title": "Why do two connected masses have one slow pattern and one fast pattern?", "explain": "In this system, two equal masses are connected by springs between two fixed walls. In one pattern the masses move together, and in the other they move in opposite directions. A third display shows the two patterns added together.", "outcome": "**The opposite-direction pattern has the higher frequency** because it stretches the middle spring more. **The mixed motion contains both modes.** Its combined movement is not a single normal mode of its own.", "interpret": "A **normal mode** is a pattern in which the whole system moves in a coordinated way at one frequency. In one normal mode, both masses share that frequency. Each mass does not have its own separate frequency."}, "modern-card-middle-mass": {"title": "Would making the middle mass heavier slow every mode?", "explain": "In a three-mass system, the middle mass moves in Modes 1 and 3. In Mode 2, the middle mass stays still while the two outer masses move. The proposed change adds mass only to the middle mass.", "outcome": "**Modes 1 and 3 go down in frequency, and Mode 2 does not change.** This holds in the ideal symmetric system. Extra inertia only matters in a mode where that mass moves.", "interpret": "This prediction comes from reading the mode patterns, not from a new measurement. It answers the Exam 2 review question about adding mass at one spot. It does not rely on how large the motion happens to look in a drawing."}, "modern-card-local-perturbation": {"title": "How can one local change push two mode frequencies in opposite directions?", "explain": "On a vibrating string, one marked spot can play different roles in different modes. In one mode the spot moves a lot. In another mode the spot stays still, but the string around it stretches strongly. The change adds both mass and stiffness at that spot at the same time.", "outcome": "The mode with strong motion at the spot **goes down in frequency** because the added mass slows it. The mode with strong stretching at the spot **goes up in frequency** because the added stiffness speeds it up.", "interpret": "**Where the spot sits in each mode decides which effect matters.** The two changes do not simply cancel out. Assuming they cancel throws away the location information that makes the prediction possible."}, "modern-card-test-voice": {"title": "Does a recorded voice have the predicted harmonic spacing?", "explain": "A physical account of the voice predicts evenly spaced **harmonics** (frequency peaks from the repeating source). Recording a steady voiced sound and looking at its **spectrum** (the strength at each frequency) tests that prediction.", "outcome": "Checking the lowest harmonic and the next one shows the expected spacing. **The important result is the regular spacing, which comes from the repeating source.** One speaker's measured pitch is not the thing to memorize.", "interpret": "The measurement links the theory to a pattern anyone can observe. It supports the predicted relationship between the source and its harmonics. The original recording and measurement can still be checked directly."}, "modern-card-end-conditions": {"title": "Why do the end conditions change which frequencies are allowed?", "explain": "A string fixed at both ends must stay still at both ends. In the fixed-free case, one end must stay still and the other end must move as much as possible.", "outcome": "Whole numbers of half-waves fit a string fixed at both ends. **Odd numbers of quarter-waves** fit the fixed-free case, so the ideal frequencies are odd multiples of the lowest one.", "interpret": "**The end conditions decide which patterns are allowed.** This comparison is about the ideal models, which have their own tuning. It does not claim that every real building has resonances at exact odd multiples."}, "modern-card-vowel-constrictions": {"title": "Why does moving a constriction change F1 and F2 differently?", "explain": "A **constriction** (narrowing) can sit near the lips for /u/, at the front for /i/, or farther back for /a/. For each location, the motion and the compression of the air are checked separately in the first two modes. **Compression** means the air particles are pushed closer together.", "outcome": "**/u/ lowers both formants, /i/ lowers F1 and raises F2, and /a/ raises F1 and lowers F2** in these models. **Formants** are resonances of the vocal tract. The same kind of narrowing has different results because it happens at different places in the modes.", "interpret": "The explanation comes from how the constriction lines up with each mode pattern. Knowing which mode moves or compresses at the narrowing lets you work out each direction. A memorized list of directions does not give that reasoning."}, "modern-card-source-filter": {"title": "Can one harmonic source produce different vowels?", "explain": "In the source-filter model, the vocal folds are the **source** and the vocal tract is the **filter**. The source supplies evenly spaced harmonic peaks. Different tract shapes favor different frequency regions, while the source spacing can stay the same.", "outcome": "**The heights of the harmonics change around the formant resonances**, so each vowel gets a different **envelope** (the overall shape traced by the peaks). The filter can change the vowel quality without moving every harmonic frequency.", "interpret": "This separates the job of the vocal folds from the job of the vocal tract. Pitch depends on how fast the source repeats. Vowel quality depends strongly on how the tract's resonances shape that source."}, "modern-card-harmonic-sampling": {"title": "Why is a formant pattern easier to read with a lower-pitched voice?", "explain": "The display applies similar vowel filters to sources with different **F0** (fundamental frequency, heard as pitch). A higher F0 leaves fewer harmonic peaks inside the same frequency range.", "outcome": "Closely spaced harmonics trace the envelope in more detail. **Widely spaced harmonics can miss a resonance region**, so the vowel pattern becomes less clear.", "interpret": "A peak that is hard to see does not mean the tract lost that resonance. The filter's response stays the same. What changes is how closely the source harmonics sample that response."}, "modern-card-spectrogram-transition": {"title": "How does a series of spectra become a spectrogram of a changing vowel?", "explain": "Each spectrum shows the frequency content of one short slice of time. A **spectrogram** lines those slices up in time order and shows stronger amplitude as darker shading.", "outcome": "The formant bands become lines that move through time. **From /i/ to /a/, F1 rises and F2 falls** as the tract moves toward the new shape.", "interpret": "The spectrogram keeps both the frequency relationships and their timing. A single spectrum on its own cannot show that timing."}, "modern-card-ma-na": {"title": "Why can the consonant from ma be heard as na after splicing?", "explain": "In this splicing test, the consonant part of ma is joined to the vowel part of na. The two pieces then give conflicting information about which consonant came first.", "outcome": "Listeners hear the result as **na**. The vowel's **formant transition** (the change in formants as the vowel begins) carries information linked to /n/. That information can win over the consonant itself.", "interpret": "Listeners use context that reaches into the following vowel. So recognition does not work by deciding each tiny slice of sound on its own."}, "modern-card-untrained-network": {"title": "What does an untrained network do with an /i/-like input?", "explain": "The two input formants match the /i/-like category dean, but the network's weights are not useful yet. A **forward pass** runs the inputs through those weights to compute a probability for each category.", "outcome": "The network favors **done instead of dean**. The output still follows the probability rules, yet its highest probability goes to the wrong category.", "interpret": "An output in the correct format is not the same as a correct classification. The **target** gives the correct category, so the network's error can guide later learning."}, "modern-card-sigmoid-network": {"title": "Does adding sigmoid leave the final prediction the same?", "explain": "The formant inputs stay the same, but each hidden weighted sum now passes through **sigmoid**, an S-shaped function. Each transformed hidden value becomes the input to the next layer.", "outcome": "The later activations and the final probabilities change. **Changing the processing rule is enough to change the decision**, even before any training happens.", "interpret": "This comparison does not show that sigmoid alone makes the classifier correct. Adding sigmoid changes how the forward pass computes. Learning the weights is a separate step."}, "modern-card-linear-training": {"title": "How much of the vowel distinction does this linear training run learn?", "explain": "The F1 and F2 data contain vowel categories that overlap. The curve tracks **training accuracy** as the linear network's weights are updated again and again.", "outcome": "Accuracy rises above the guessing level of about one in five and levels off at **44.95 percent**. This curve comes from an equivalent NumPy reference run. It is not output from running the PyTorch code.", "interpret": "The run learns useful distinctions, but it still gets many training examples wrong. The final percentage belongs to this dataset, network design and training procedure. It is not a general limit on vowel recognition."}, "modern-card-repeated-training": {"title": "Do different random starting weights lead to different final accuracies?", "explain": "Ten runs use the same training setup, but each one starts from different random weights. Their accuracy curves are compared over the same number of updates.", "outcome": "The curves take different paths, but **all ten runs end at 44.95 percent training accuracy**. So in this setup, different starting weights do not force different final results.", "interpret": "Getting the same result many times makes the finding stronger for this experiment. It does not guarantee the same result for every starting point, program or new dataset."}, "modern-card-letter-game": {"title": "What can the next-letter game reveal about knowledge of English?", "explain": "In the next-letter game, a word beginning such as sp is given. The player then picks a next letter that could continue a real word. Some beginnings allow many choices, while others allow only a few.", "outcome": "The accepted letters follow the onset and spelling patterns of English. An **onset** is the group of consonants before a syllable's vowel. **Predicting well requires sensitivity to the pattern**, even when nobody states the rule.", "interpret": "The game motivates the idea of learning by completing sequences. It does not report the measured accuracy of a trained language model. Its examples are spellings, so they are not full phonetic transcriptions."}}, "contrasts": {"modern-contrast-mass-stiffness": {"title": "Mass and stiffness change frequency in opposite directions.", "left": {"term": "Mass", "text": "**Mass resists changes in motion.** With stiffness held the same, more mass makes the system speed up and turn around more slowly."}, "right": {"term": "Stiffness", "text": "**Stiffness sets how hard the spring pulls back when it is stretched.** With mass held the same, more stiffness gives a stronger pull for the same stretch."}, "paragraphs": ["More mass lowers the frequency, and more stiffness raises it. To predict what happens when both change, you need the size of each effect. In a system with many parts, you also need to know where each change happens.", "A heavy mass takes longer to turn around. A stiffer spring turns the same mass around faster. A large movement only describes amplitude, so it does not tell which system cycles faster."]}, "modern-contrast-node-antinode": {"title": "A point that stays still can still have strong spring action.", "left": {"term": "Displacement node", "text": "A **displacement node** is a point that **stays still** in that mode. Motion on either side of it can still stretch or compress the material there strongly."}, "right": {"term": "Displacement antinode", "text": "A **displacement antinode** is the point with **the most motion**. In this standing-wave model, the stretching is smallest there."}, "paragraphs": ["Added mass has little or no effect at an exact node because that point does not move. Added stiffness matters where the mode stretches or compresses. So a point that stays still can still matter.", "The same marked spot can be a node in one mode and an antinode in another. Reading each mode separately explains why one change can push frequencies in opposite directions."]}, "modern-contrast-motion-compression": {"title": "Air motion and air compression are different measurements.", "left": {"term": "Motion", "text": "**Displacement is how far a particle moves from its resting position.** Large displacement means strong back-and-forth motion."}, "right": {"term": "Compression", "text": "**Compression is a change in how closely particles are packed together.** It can be strong at a place where the particles themselves barely move."}, "paragraphs": ["How far one particle moves is different from how the gap between neighboring particles changes. A constriction's effect depends on which of these is stronger for that mode at that location.", "For /i/, the front constriction sits where Mode 1 mainly moves and Mode 2 mainly compresses. So F1 goes down and F2 goes up."]}, "modern-contrast-mode-mixture": {"title": "A normal mode is one organized pattern, and a mixture combines several.", "left": {"term": "Normal mode", "text": "A **normal mode** is one coordinated pattern of motion with its own frequency. The pattern describes the whole system at once."}, "right": {"term": "Mixture of modes", "text": "A **mixture** adds together several normal modes. Its visible movement can look more complicated than any single mode."}, "paragraphs": ["Seeing complicated motion does not mean there is an extra normal mode. A spectrum can show which mode frequencies add up to make the combined movement.", "The lower row of the two-mass display combines both modes. So it should not be given just one of the two mode frequencies."]}, "modern-contrast-boundary-types": {"title": "The allowed frequencies follow from what the ends must do.", "left": {"term": "Both ends fixed", "text": "Both ends must have **zero displacement**. An ideal uniform string fits whole numbers of half-waves, so its harmonics are whole-number multiples."}, "right": {"term": "One fixed end and one free end", "text": "One end stays still while the other has the **greatest displacement**. The ideal model fits odd numbers of quarter-waves."}, "paragraphs": ["The whole-number series and the odd-number series come from different allowed patterns. Counting the wave pieces explains each series, so it does not need to be memorized.", "A string fixed at both ends with a 200 Hz first mode has its fifth mode at 1,000 Hz. A neutral fixed-free tube with a 500 Hz first resonance has its next resonance at 1,500 Hz."]}, "modern-contrast-source-filter": {"title": "Harmonics come from the source, and formants come from the filter.", "left": {"term": "Harmonics", "text": "**Harmonics are whole-number multiples of the source's fundamental frequency.** Their spacing depends on how fast the source repeats."}, "right": {"term": "Formants", "text": "**Formants are resonances of the vocal tract.** Their positions depend strongly on the shape of the tract."}, "paragraphs": ["The filter changes how strong each source harmonic is. A formant does not have to sit exactly on a harmonic. A formant's number is also not the number of a harmonic.", "Changing from /i/ to /a/ can move the formants while F0 stays the same. Raising F0 spreads the harmonics apart while the vowel filter stays about the same."]}, "modern-contrast-pitch-vowel": {"title": "Pitch and vowel quality can change separately.", "left": {"term": "Pitch", "text": "The **F0** of the repeating voiced source is closely tied to pitch. Faster vocal-fold vibration spreads the harmonics farther apart."}, "right": {"term": "Vowel quality", "text": "The tract's **formant pattern** strongly shapes which vowel is heard. Moving the tongue or lips changes that pattern."}, "paragraphs": ["A higher-pitched /i/ does not turn into /a/. Still, widely spaced harmonics at a high F0 can make the formants harder to find. So the two mechanisms are separate, but hearing them is not always perfectly separate.", "A singer can keep the same vowel while changing the note. At very high pitches, few harmonics fall near a formant, so the vowel can become less clear."]}, "modern-contrast-i-a": {"title": "The /i/ and /a/ constrictions reverse the two main effects.", "left": {"term": "The /i/ configuration", "text": "The front constriction sits where Mode 1 moves and Mode 2 compresses. **F1 falls and F2 rises.**"}, "right": {"term": "The /a/ configuration", "text": "The constriction farther back meets the two modes in the opposite roles. **F1 rises and F2 falls.**"}, "paragraphs": ["The effects of narrowing are not random for each vowel. The results differ because the same kinds of change happen at different places in the mode patterns.", "Moving from /i/ toward /a/ should bring F1 and F2 closer together. This happens because the main effects of inertia and stiffness switch between the two modes."]}, "modern-contrast-u-i": {"title": "Two high vowels can have different second formants.", "left": {"term": "The /u/ configuration", "text": "The narrowing near the lips falls on strong motion in both of the first two modes. **F1 and F2 both fall** in this comparison."}, "right": {"term": "The /i/ configuration", "text": "A front constriction affects motion in the first mode and compression in the second. **F1 falls and F2 rises.**"}, "paragraphs": ["Calling both vowels high describes the height of the tongue. It does not mean they have the same acoustic pattern. Lip shape and the place of narrowing affect the second mode differently.", "The vowels in dune and dean are both high vowels, but they sit in different parts of an F1 and F2 plot. The second formant helps tell them apart."]}, "modern-contrast-spectrum-spectrogram": {"title": "A spectrum shows one moment, and a spectrogram shows change over time.", "left": {"term": "Spectrum", "text": "A **spectrum** shows the frequency content of one short time window. Frequency runs from left to right, and height shows amplitude."}, "right": {"term": "Spectrogram", "text": "A **spectrogram** lines up many time windows in order. Frequency runs from bottom to top, and darkness shows amplitude."}, "paragraphs": ["The two displays show related information arranged in different ways. A high point on a spectrum means strong amplitude. A high position on a spectrogram means high frequency.", "On a spectrogram, a changing vowel appears as formant bands that move. One spectrum cannot tell whether those resonances had just risen or were about to fall."]}, "modern-contrast-input-target": {"title": "The input describes the sound, and the target names its correct category.", "left": {"term": "Input", "text": "The **input row** holds the measured F1 and F2 values. The network receives these measurements and uses them to classify the vowel."}, "right": {"term": "Target", "text": "The **target row** puts a one in the correct vowel's column and zeros everywhere else. It gives the correct answer for training."}, "paragraphs": ["The target is not another measured formant, and it does not replace the input. Its job is to show the gap between the network's prediction and the known vowel.", "An /i/-like formant row goes with the dean target. Shuffling one table without the other would teach the wrong pairings."]}, "modern-contrast-valid-correct": {"title": "A valid probability distribution can still choose the wrong answer.", "left": {"term": "Valid output", "text": "A **valid probability distribution** has no negative entries, and its entries add up to one. These rules describe only its mathematical form."}, "right": {"term": "Correct decision", "text": "A **correct classification** gives the most support to the real category of the input. Accuracy is about whether the prediction matches the known category."}, "paragraphs": ["Following the probability rules does not show that the weights have learned useful categories. A distribution that favors the wrong answer can still be perfectly well formed.", "The untrained network favors done for an input whose target is dean. That is a classification error. The entries still add up to one, so the form of the output is fine."]}, "modern-contrast-activation-weight": {"title": "An activation changes with the input, and a weight controls a connection.", "left": {"term": "Activation", "text": "An **activation** is a value produced or received during processing. It changes when the input or earlier calculations change."}, "right": {"term": "Weight", "text": "A **weight** sets how much a connection contributes to the receiving neuron. It is a learned value used during a forward pass."}, "paragraphs": ["A large activation is not the same as a large learned weight. The receiving neuron multiplies each incoming activation by its connection weight and combines the results.", "Changing F1 can change the hidden activations without changing any weights. A training update changes the weights, so it can change how later inputs are processed."]}, "modern-contrast-inference-learning": {"title": "Inference uses the weights, and learning changes them.", "left": {"term": "Inference", "text": "**Inference** computes a decision with the current weights. It works the same way whether the weights are useful or untrained."}, "right": {"term": "Learning", "text": "**Learning** uses feedback to improve the weights. It needs a comparison with a target or another training goal."}, "paragraphs": ["A forward pass is needed to find the network's current mistake. Running it again with the same inputs and weights does not fix the mistake.", "The untrained network already produces a full set of probabilities. Training matters because the target and the error guide changes in the connections behind those probabilities."]}, "modern-contrast-gradient-update": {"title": "Backpropagation and SGD do different parts of an update.", "left": {"term": "Backpropagation", "text": "**Backpropagation computes gradients** by passing error information backward through the calculations that depend on each other. A **gradient** tells how the loss changes when a weight changes."}, "right": {"term": "SGD", "text": "**SGD (stochastic gradient descent) changes the weights** using the gradients and a learning rate. SGD is the optimizer, and it does not do the backward calculation itself."}, "paragraphs": ["The backward calculation reuses work already done for later parts of the network, so it is linked to **dynamic programming**. The optimizer then decides how far to move in the direction the gradients give.", "loss.backward() computes the gradients, and the optimizer's step applies them. Calling both the same operation hides the difference between finding a correction and making it."]}, "modern-contrast-near-far-error": {"title": "A final connection has a shorter path to the error than an early one.", "left": {"term": "Near the output", "text": "A final-layer weight affects the output through only a few calculations. So its influence is fairly direct."}, "right": {"term": "Early in the network", "text": "An early weight changes middle activations that pass through more connections. So its final influence depends on all those later paths."}, "paragraphs": ["This dependence explains why the backward calculation runs in reverse order. Reusing the solved later derivatives makes the early weights manageable. Each connection's effect does not have to be guessed on its own.", "A positive input to a weight that feeds the output directly gives a clear local sign. The sign of an early weight alone does not tell its effect on every final category."]}, "modern-contrast-hidden-output": {"title": "Hidden layers process the information, and the output layer names the categories.", "left": {"term": "Hidden layers", "text": "**Hidden layers** transform the information between the input and the final decision. Their neurons are not extra category labels."}, "right": {"term": "Output layer", "text": "The **output layer** gives one value for each answer category. In the vowel network it has five positions."}, "paragraphs": ["So four processing layers can contain only three hidden layers, because the last processing layer is the output layer.", "A network with processing sizes of three, two, four and five has three hidden groups. Adding a new group of three neurons before the last layer adds a hidden layer. The network still has five output categories."]}, "modern-contrast-linear-nonlinear": {"title": "A nonlinear step changes what stacked weighted sums can represent.", "left": {"term": "Purely linear layers", "text": "A chain of **linear transformations** can always be combined into one linear transformation. So adding more linear layers alone cannot make a nonlinear boundary."}, "right": {"term": "Sigmoid between layers", "text": "**Sigmoid** passes each weighted sum through an S-shaped function. Using it between layers makes the whole calculation nonlinear."}, "paragraphs": ["The difference comes from the operations, not just the number of neurons. A nonlinear step changes what later layers receive, so every later calculation must use the new values.", "Keeping the same formants and adding sigmoid changes the later activations and probabilities. This change in processing is separate from an optimizer learning new weights."]}, "modern-contrast-bounded-normalized": {"title": "Being between zero and one is different from being a probability distribution.", "left": {"term": "Hidden sigmoid values", "text": "Each **sigmoid activation** lies between zero and one. Several of these values do not have to add up to one."}, "right": {"term": "Category probabilities", "text": "A **probability distribution** also has no negative values, but its values must add up to one across all the choices."}, "paragraphs": ["Sharing the same number range does not give the two the same job. Making the values add up to one across the choices is called **normalization**, and separate hidden activations do not have it.", "Three hidden values near one can all be valid sigmoid activations. They would fail as probabilities for three competing categories because their total would be more than one."]}, "modern-contrast-loss-accuracy": {"title": "Loss and accuracy measure different things about a prediction.", "left": {"term": "Loss", "text": "**Loss** measures how far the prediction is from the training target. Its changes can guide the weight updates."}, "right": {"term": "Accuracy", "text": "**Accuracy** is the fraction of category decisions that are correct. It does not fully show how confident each answer was."}, "paragraphs": ["Two predictions can pick the same category but give it different amounts of support. So their accuracy can match while their losses differ.", "A flat stretch of accuracy does not mean every weight or probability stayed the same. The decision can stay the same while the model's numbers keep shifting."]}, "modern-contrast-training-generalization": {"title": "Doing well on the training examples does not prove doing well on new recordings.", "left": {"term": "Training accuracy", "text": "**Training accuracy** measures decisions on the examples used for training."}, "right": {"term": "Generalization", "text": "**Generalization** is about decisions on new examples. Testing it needs evidence beyond the training examples."}, "paragraphs": ["This run supports a claim about this data and this training procedure. It cannot show the same percentage for new speakers or different recording conditions.", "Overlapping categories help explain why these formant measurements are hard to classify. Testing new speakers would answer a different question from whether training accuracy improved."]}, "modern-contrast-heads-layers": {"title": "Parallel heads and stacked layers add different kinds of processing.", "left": {"term": "Heads", "text": "**Heads work side by side within one layer.** Each head contributes information about different relationships."}, "right": {"term": "Layers", "text": "**Layers work one after another.** Each layer transforms information that already includes the earlier processing."}, "paragraphs": ["Adding a head side by side is different from adding another step after the existing ones. The diagram uses both arrangements, so heads and layers cannot be counted as the same thing.", "Several heads can contribute to one layer's result. The next layer then receives that combined result, so it is not just one more head in the same step."]}, "modern-contrast-heads-agents": {"title": "A head works inside a model, and an agent does a task in a larger system.", "left": {"term": "Attention head", "text": "An **attention head** is a calculating part inside one layer of a model. Its contribution is combined with the model's other internal processing."}, "right": {"term": "Agent", "text": "An **agent** is a model given a role and linked to other tasks by messages. A critic and a reviser are roles in that larger system."}, "paragraphs": ["A checking loop between models is not just another attention head. The graph of the whole system and the layers inside one model are different levels of explanation.", "A critic can ask for a draft to be revised before it is returned. That exchange of messages happens outside the model's hidden activation calculations."]}, "modern-contrast-letters-sounds": {"title": "A spelling prediction can test a pattern without being a phonetic transcription.", "left": {"term": "Letters", "text": "**Letters** are written symbols. Letter sequences make a practical word-completion exercise."}, "right": {"term": "Speech sounds", "text": "**Speech sounds** are the units people say and hear. The rules for sound sequences do not match every spelling pattern."}, "paragraphs": ["The next-letter game can reveal useful patterns. Its letters do not always match the sounds, so each example needs care. A written letter does not always stand for a spoken consonant.", "Written k begins the word knee, but the spoken word does not begin with /k/. So knee does not show that the spoken onset /kn/ is allowed in the same way."]}}, "hard": {"modern-hard-local-roles": {"title": "Why can a still point matter more for stiffness than a moving point?", "paragraphs": ["**A mode pattern describes motion over a whole cycle**, not where one dot happens to be at one moment. A **displacement node** stays still for the whole cycle. An **antinode** moves back and forth through a large range.", "Stiffness matters where neighboring parts stretch or compress against each other. Around a node, the neighbors can move strongly toward and away from each other. So a node can have strong spring action even though it does not move itself.", "Adding mass at an antinode adds inertia where the mode already moves a lot. Adding stiffness where the material stretches strongly makes the pull back to rest stronger. So the two changes act on different properties of the location.", "At the marked spot, one mode moves and another mode has a node. **Read the spot's role in each mode first.** Then predict a lower frequency from inertia or a higher frequency from stiffness."], "question": "Does a point crossing its resting position for a moment have to be a displacement node?", "answer": "No, a point crossing its resting position does not have to be a node. An antinode passes through its resting position in every cycle. **A node stays still for the entire mode**, which is a different condition."}, "modern-hard-odd-series": {"title": "Why does the fixed-free series skip the even multiples?", "paragraphs": ["A fixed-free system has two end rules. One end must stay still, and the other end must reach the greatest displacement in the mode.", "The shortest shape that meets both rules is **one quarter-wave**. Adding one more quarter-wave would put a still point at the free end. That breaks the rule for the free end.", "Adding two quarter-waves brings the free end back to full motion. So the allowed counts are one, three, five and other odd numbers. These counts give the ideal series of odd multiples.", "The shape explains the series, so it does not need to be memorized as a list. A string held still at both ends has a different rule, so it can fit every whole number of half-waves."], "question": "What should you check first before giving a drawing a harmonic series?", "answer": "First check **what each end is allowed to do**. The allowed patterns depend on whether each end must stay still or move. So the end conditions decide which series fits."}, "modern-hard-air-drawing": {"title": "How can a wave-shaped drawing show air moving along the tract?", "paragraphs": ["The air particles move back and forth **along the vocal tract**, which is called **longitudinal** motion. A graph still draws their displacement up and down, so the size of the motion at each point is easy to see.", "The drawing shows an amount at each point, not a real path between the roof and floor of the mouth. A tall value means the air moves a long way along the tract at that location.", "Compression depends on how neighboring particles move toward or away from each other. Around a point with little displacement, the particles on each side can crowd together or spread apart strongly. That changes the air pressure there.", "Keeping displacement and compression separate makes the vowel predictions make sense. A narrowing can slow a moving region through inertia. It can also raise the frequency of a compressing mode through added stiffness."], "question": "Why is the highest point on a displacement curve not automatically the point of greatest compression?", "answer": "The highest point is where particles move the most, which is not where they are packed most tightly. Its height shows **particle displacement**. Compression shows how the gaps between neighbors change, and the two follow different patterns along the tract."}, "modern-hard-source-sampling": {"title": "Why can the same vowel filter look different at different pitches?", "paragraphs": ["A tract resonance is a frequency region that the filter favors. The source only provides harmonics at multiples of its current F0. So those harmonics sample the filter's response at separate frequencies.", "At a low F0, harmonics are close together, so they fall on both sides of a resonance and show its broad shape. At a high F0, the next harmonic can be far away. A formant can then fall in the gap between harmonics.", "When that region is poorly sampled, the filter still has its resonance. **The source pattern and the filter response each contribute separately** to the spectrum you see.", "This explains how source and filter relate, and why some very high-pitched vowels are less clear. It also keeps a harmonic's frequency from being mistaken for the exact position of a formant."], "question": "Does the strongest visible harmonic always give the exact formant frequency?", "answer": "No, the strongest harmonic does not always mark the exact formant frequency. It may just be the harmonic that lies nearest the resonance. **The resonance itself can sit between two harmonics.**"}, "modern-hard-backward-order": {"title": "Why does working backward solve a forward network's learning problem?", "paragraphs": ["The forward pass computes the values that make up a prediction. Comparing the prediction with the target gives an error. The error alone does not show how each earlier connection contributed.", "A final connection sits close to the error. An earlier connection acts through several later activations, so its effect depends on calculations that come after it.", "**Backpropagation solves those later dependencies first and reuses them.** That is why **dynamic programming**, the same method used for shortest-path problems, explains its efficiency.", "The backward pass calculates gradients from the values stored during the forward pass. The optimizer then uses those gradients to update the weights. So calculating the gradients and changing the weights are separate steps."], "question": "Are the final-layer weights changed before each earlier gradient is calculated?", "answer": "No, the weights are not changed until the backward calculation is finished. The backward pass uses the forward-pass values and the current weights, so all the gradients fit together. **The weight update comes after that calculation**, so earlier gradients are never rebuilt from an already changed network."}, "modern-hard-weight-sign": {"title": "Why is “increase the weight” not a complete prediction?", "paragraphs": ["A connection contributes its incoming activation multiplied by its weight. So the sign of the activation matters before you can tell what a larger weight does.", "For a positive activation going straight into an output, a larger weight raises that output's raw score. For a negative activation, the same increase pushes the contribution the other way.", "Whether that change helps also depends on the target. An output with too much support should be lowered. An output with too little support needs a different correction.", "For an early hidden connection, the change also passes through later paths and transformations. **Follow the sign, the target and the later connections** before calling an increase an improvement."], "question": "Which condition makes increasing a direct output weight raise that output's raw score?", "answer": "The incoming activation must be **positive**, with the other contributions held the same. The sign of the activation sets the local direction. So a positive activation times a larger weight gives a larger raw score."}, "modern-hard-hidden-count": {"title": "Why do four Linear layers mean three hidden layers?", "paragraphs": ["The two input values are measurements given to the network. They do not need a Linear declaration just to exist as inputs.", "Each **Linear declaration** creates one processing layer. Here, three layers produce middle activations, and the last layer produces values for five vowel categories.", "Only the middle layers are **hidden layers**. Counting the final decision layer as hidden would put one processing step in the wrong group.", "Adding three neurons just before the output needs a connection from four to three. It then needs a connection from three to five. The sizes must match where layers meet, and the two input measurements stay the same."], "question": "Does adding a hidden layer require adding another measured formant input?", "answer": "No, adding a hidden layer does not need another formant input. It adds an **internal processing step** inside the network. The input can still be just F1 and F2."}, "modern-hard-sigmoid-distribution": {"title": "Why is a value between zero and one not automatically a probability?", "paragraphs": ["Sigmoid turns one weighted sum into a number between zero and one. Applying it to several neurons separately gives several such numbers.", "A distribution over competing categories has one more rule. **The category probabilities must add up to one together**, and separate sigmoid activations do not guarantee that.", "The hidden values can be useful features without being category decisions. Their job is to give the next processing step something to work with.", "Sigmoid also adds a nonlinear step between the weighted sums. That changes the function the network computes, so all later activations must be recomputed from the transformed values."], "question": "Can three hidden sigmoid neurons all be close to one?", "answer": "Yes, three hidden sigmoid neurons can all be close to one. Each activation is kept between zero and one on its own. **Their sum does not have to equal one**, because they are not probabilities for competing categories."}, "modern-hard-spelling-boundaries": {"title": "Why can a cluster work inside a word but fail at the start of a syllable?", "paragraphs": ["Sound rules apply to positions inside syllables, not just to a row of written consonants. A syllable has an **onset** before its vowel and a **coda** after it.", "Inside a longer word, a group of consonants can cross a syllable boundary. The first consonant can end one syllable while the next begins another. That avoids a combination that would not be allowed in one onset.", "The next-letter game makes some of these expectations visible by limiting how a word beginning can continue. A model that predicts well must learn which continuations fit the context.", "**Letters are still different from sounds**, so spellings such as knee need care. They can test knowledge of written patterns. They do not show that every written cluster is pronounced as an onset."], "question": "Why should a written k followed by n not be treated as proof of a spoken /kn/ onset?", "answer": "A written kn is not proof of a spoken /kn/ onset because the k in knee is not pronounced. **The spelling and the spoken sound sequence are different representations.** So a written cluster does not show which spoken onsets are allowed."}}, "connections": {"modern-link-top-lips": {"title": "The free top of a building helps explain narrowing near the lips.", "paragraphs": ["A building is held at its base, while its top is free to move. The neutral vocal-tract model gives its air column a matching **closed end and open end**. So the end conditions line up, even though the materials differ.", "In the ideal comparison, adding mass at the free top slows the building's mode. In the same way, narrowing the tract at the lips, where the air moves strongly, lowers the first two resonances in the /u/ model.", "What carries over is **where the change acts within the mode**. The comparison does not require air to bend sideways. It also does not require real buildings to have the same resonance ratios as a uniform tube."], "question": "Why would moving the load away from the free end require a new prediction?", "answer": "Moving the load needs a new prediction because the new spot can have different motion and stretching in each mode. **The result at the free end cannot just be copied to another location.** Each mode has to be checked again at the new spot."}, "modern-link-measurement-chain": {"title": "A physical speech mechanism can become information for a classifier.", "paragraphs": ["Vocal-fold vibration provides the repeating source, and the vocal tract shapes how strong its harmonics are. Measuring the resulting sound gives information about the tract's resonances.", "**F1 and F2 become number inputs** to the vowel network. The network then uses its learned weights to turn those measurements into support for each possible category.", "The measurement is not itself a category label. The tract shape, the sound measurement and the network's decision are separate stages. Physical causes link the first two, and calculation links the last two."], "question": "Why can an F1 and F2 measurement be sensible even when the network labels it incorrectly?", "answer": "The measurement can be correct while the decision is wrong because the two have different jobs. The measurement describes the sound, and the weights turn it into a decision. **Poor weights can misclassify a valid measurement.**"}, "modern-link-patterns-spectrum": {"title": "A mode mixture connects visible motion with a spectrum.", "paragraphs": ["Several normal modes can add up in the movement recorded at one location. Their **superposition** (adding together) can produce a complicated time trace. Each mode on its own is still an organized pattern.", "A spectrum separates these contributions by frequency. **The complicated trace and its frequency parts describe the same combined signal** in two different forms.", "How much a mode contributes also depends on where the measurement is taken. A point that is a node for one mode cannot show strong motion from that mode."], "question": "Why can a single pickup fail to reveal one mode strongly?", "answer": "A single pickup can miss a mode when it sits near that mode's **displacement node**. That mode barely moves the point there, so its share of the recorded movement is small."}, "modern-link-perturbation-inverse": {"title": "A frequency shift can reveal what a location was doing.", "paragraphs": ["A forward prediction starts with a location's role and predicts which way the frequency changes. A region with strong motion responds strongly to added inertia. A region with strong compression responds strongly to added stiffness.", "The same reasoning also works in reverse. **A large upward shift in a resonance points to a strong stiffness effect** at the changed location for that mode.", "This conclusion applies to only one mode, because another mode can use the same location differently. A result about F2 should not be quietly applied to F1."], "question": "What would a downward shift suggest under the same constriction model?", "answer": "A downward shift suggests that **the inertia effect from motion is stronger** for that mode at that location. Added inertia slows the motion there, so the frequency drops."}, "modern-link-context-speech": {"title": "Speech recognition and sequence prediction both benefit from context.", "paragraphs": ["A following vowel can carry information about the consonant before it. In the splicing example, the evidence for the consonant reaches beyond one isolated sound.", "A sequence model also uses context when choosing a continuation. **The relations among items carry information that no single item has alone.**", "The two cases show the value of context at different levels. The splicing result does not show that human speech perception uses any particular language-model design."], "question": "What is lost when every input fragment is classified independently?", "answer": "Classifying each fragment alone loses **the relationships across fragments**. These include transitions and context rules. Those relationships can help decide what a sound is."}, "modern-link-backward-routes": {"title": "Backpropagation reuses solved steps the way a shortest-path method does.", "paragraphs": ["In a shortest-path problem, the best value for the rest of a route can be solved once. Many earlier routes can then use that value. Solving the same remaining route again for each path would repeat work.", "An early connection in a network also depends on later calculations. **Backpropagation solves those later steps once and reuses them.** It does not trace every full route from every early weight to the error.", "The shared idea is **dynamic programming** over calculations that depend on each other. In the network, the number passed back is a derivative of the loss. It is not the distance left on a road."], "question": "Why is the comparison useful even though a derivative is not a route cost?", "answer": "The comparison is useful because both problems reuse **shared solved subproblems**. The way the work is organized carries over from one problem to the other. The two quantities themselves are still different."}, "modern-link-state-knowledge": {"title": "Current activations and learned weights carry different kinds of information.", "paragraphs": ["Activations are the values being processed for the current input and context. Weights decide how those values affect the next calculation.", "A **recurrent state** can carry information from one step to the next without replacing the learned weights. **What is being processed right now is different from the weights that organize the processing.**", "Learning changes the weights using feedback. Ordinary inference can change activations again and again while the weights stay fixed. This difference still holds as models get deeper or handle longer sequences."], "question": "Does a different activation pattern prove that learning occurred?", "answer": "No, a new activation pattern does not prove that learning happened. A new input or context changes the activations during **inference with unchanged weights**. Learning only happens when the weights change."}, "modern-link-time-depth": {"title": "Repeated transformation links physical motion over time with network depth.", "paragraphs": ["A physical oscillator updates its state again and again over time. A deep model transforms its information again and again as it passes through layers.", "Both cases need **the current state and the rule that produces the next state**. Seeing them this way makes a changing set of numbers easier to follow as a process.", "The comparison has limits because different network layers can have different weights. Repeated transformation does not need a real physical oscillator. It also does not need one identical matrix reused at every step."], "question": "What remains useful when the rule differs from layer to layer?", "answer": "The idea that **each step's output becomes the next step's input state** stays useful. This holds even when each layer has its own weights."}, "modern-link-completion-learning": {"title": "The next-letter game is a small example of learning from completion.", "paragraphs": ["A word beginning limits which letters can follow. So predicting the next letter correctly requires sensitivity to patterns already in the sequence.", "A model can learn from the gap between its predicted probabilities and the actual next item. **Completion turns language patterns into training information**, so no one has to state every rule in words.", "What the model receives still matters. Letter patterns can reflect spelling as well as sound structure. So success at completion should be judged against what the model's input actually is."], "question": "Why is a broad next-letter distribution sometimes appropriate?", "answer": "A broad distribution fits when many continuations are possible. Some beginnings really do allow many next letters. **Knowing the language can mean being uncertain**, so the model does not always need one strongly favored answer."}, "modern-link-feedback-checking": {"title": "A critic and reviser loop adds feedback at the level of the whole system.", "paragraphs": ["An **agentic system** can give drafting and checking to different models. The critic's feedback then shapes the next revision, so it is not thrown away after the first answer.", "**The loop runs between models doing tasks**, while attention heads and hidden layers work inside a model. Keeping these levels separate makes the diagram's organization clear.", "A checking step can improve an answer, but it still needs evidence of what it caught. Having a critic is a procedure. It does not guarantee that every remaining statement is true."], "question": "How could the checking process be evaluated?", "answer": "The checking process can be evaluated by comparing its claims with **independent source evidence**. It also helps to check whether the revisions really fixed the problems the critic found."}}, "figures": {"modern-figure-mass-rate": {"title": "Compare how many cycles each system completes in the same time.", "takeaway": "**The slower row is the heavier system, and the faster row is the stiffer system.** Only one thing changes in each row. How big the drawn movement is does not affect the number of cycles."}, "modern-figure-two-patterns": {"title": "Read the same-direction pattern and the opposite-direction pattern.", "takeaway": "**Opposite movement stretches the middle spring more.** The combined row adds the two patterns together, so it is not a third normal mode."}, "modern-figure-three-patterns": {"title": "Find the middle mass that stays still in Mode 2.", "takeaway": "**In Mode 2 the middle mass stays still** while the outer masses move. Modes 1 and 3 move the middle mass, so adding mass there changes their frequencies."}, "modern-figure-mode-atlas": {"title": "Keep the number of masses separate from the mode number.", "takeaway": "**A row with more moving masses allows more mode patterns.** Each column shows one particular mode. It does not mean a new object is added to the same system."}, "modern-figure-fixed-packets": {"title": "Fit half-waves between two ends that stay still.", "takeaway": "**Every whole number of half-waves keeps both ends still.** The animation builds each pattern piece by piece, so the harmonic spacing is explained and not just listed."}, "modern-figure-membrane-patterns": {"title": "Follow the motion in more than one direction on a vibrating surface.", "takeaway": "**A pickup records all the modes added together at one spot.** If the spot is a node for one mode, that mode will not move it much."}, "modern-figure-odd-packets": {"title": "Fit quarter-waves without making the free end a node.", "takeaway": "**One, three, five or any odd number of quarter-waves** ends at the required moving point. The original construction animation is still available, and it shows steps the still image leaves out."}, "modern-figure-air-displacement": {"title": "Tell the air's movement along the tract apart from how it is drawn.", "takeaway": "**The vertical curve shows how far the air moves at each point in the tract.** It does not mean the air particles travel up and down through the tract walls."}, "modern-figure-vowel-mri": {"title": "Connect tongue and lip shapes with the place of narrowing.", "takeaway": "**The place of narrowing differs across vowels**, so the two air modes meet it in different roles. The anatomical image shows the tract shape. The mode model explains what that shape does to the sound."}, "modern-figure-boxed-string": {"title": "Check the same boxed spot in both mode patterns.", "takeaway": "**In the box, one mode moves strongly while the other mostly stretches.** So a local change has to be read twice, once for each mode."}, "modern-figure-u-loading": {"title": "Read the narrowing near the lips in both of the first two modes.", "takeaway": "**Both modes move strongly near the lips**, so added inertia lowers both frequencies. This explains why F1 and F2 are both low in this comparison."}, "modern-figure-i-front": {"title": "Use one constriction location to explain shifts in opposite directions.", "takeaway": "**At the /i/ narrowing, Mode 1 mainly moves and Mode 2 mainly compresses.** The two formants move in opposite directions because of those different roles."}, "modern-figure-a-back": {"title": "Check how a narrowing farther back reverses the two roles.", "takeaway": "**At the /a/ location, compression is stronger in the first mode and motion is stronger in the second.** So F1 rises and F2 falls in this comparison."}, "modern-figure-harmonic-comb": {"title": "Read the source peaks as an evenly spaced harmonic comb.", "takeaway": "**A higher F0 leaves wider gaps between peaks in the same frequency range.** This changes how the tract filter is sampled, but it does not turn any peak into a formant."}, "modern-figure-neutral-filter": {"title": "Compare the source peaks with the envelope of the neutral vowel.", "takeaway": "**The harmonic frequencies stay set by the source, while their heights follow the filter.** The broad envelope gives information about the tract's resonances."}, "modern-figure-i-envelope": {"title": "Recognize the low first formant and high second formant of /i/.", "takeaway": "**The /i/ envelope favors a low F1 region and a high F2 region that are far apart.** Several harmonics can contribute near each resonance, so a formant is not one single numbered harmonic."}, "modern-figure-a-envelope": {"title": "Compare the closer first and second formants of /a/.", "takeaway": "**The first two formant regions of /a/ are closer together than those of /i/.** Their positions come from the different constriction, and the meaning of F0 stays the same."}, "modern-figure-source-grid": {"title": "Keep source changes in the rows separate from filter changes in the columns.", "takeaway": "**The rows change the harmonic spacing of the source, and the columns change the vowel filter.** The grid is useful because it varies these two parts separately."}, "modern-figure-spectrogram": {"title": "Follow frequency bands through time, and do not read height as loudness.", "takeaway": "**The rising first band and the falling second band follow the change from /i/ to /a/.** Darkness shows amplitude, and the height of a band shows its frequency."}, "modern-figure-network-shape": {"title": "Count the hidden groups and follow the connections into each neuron.", "takeaway": "**The three hidden groups have three, two and four neurons.** Each receiving neuron has one incoming weight for each value in the group before it."}, "modern-figure-forward-values": {"title": "Follow the same two input measurements to the final category decision.", "takeaway": "**The network favors done even though the target is dean.** So finishing a valid forward calculation is not the same as having learned an accurate classifier."}, "modern-figure-error-directions": {"title": "Compare a marked final connection with a marked early connection.", "takeaway": "**The final connection has a more direct path to the output error.** The early connection affects the error through the activations and weights that come after it."}, "modern-figure-paired-tables": {"title": "Keep each formant row lined up with its target row.", "takeaway": "**The input table holds the measurements, and the target table names the categories.** A one in the dean column belongs to the recording labeled dean. It does not belong to whatever input lands in that row after shuffling one table alone."}, "modern-figure-sigmoid-curve": {"title": "Read the middle and the ends of the S-shaped curve.", "takeaway": "**A weighted sum near zero gives a middle activation**, while very large positive or negative sums get close to the limits. The curve's slope changes along its length. So the same change in input can have different effects at different places on the curve."}, "modern-figure-sigmoid-network": {"title": "Tell the original weighted sum apart from the hidden value after sigmoid.", "takeaway": "**The later connections must use the value after sigmoid.** If the later values stayed the same, they would not describe the network with sigmoid added."}, "modern-figure-vowel-scatter": {"title": "Read each point as one labeled vowel recording.", "takeaway": "**The horizontal position is F1 and the vertical position is F2**, and the marking shows the vowel category. Where categories overlap, nearby measurements can belong to different vowels."}, "modern-figure-training-curve": {"title": "Read accuracy as the share of correct training decisions.", "takeaway": "**The curve rises from about the guessing level for five categories and levels off at 44.95 percent.** The axes show progress over updates and training accuracy. They do not show how loud or clear the vowels were."}, "modern-figure-restart-curves": {"title": "Compare different paths that reach the same endpoint.", "takeaway": "**All the runs reach the same final training accuracy, even though their early curves differ.** So the path can vary while the final share correct stays the same."}, "modern-figure-recurrent-state": {"title": "Find the information passed from one processing step to the next.", "takeaway": "**Feedback carries context into the next calculation.** So a recurrent state is not just another separate classification of one isolated input."}, "modern-figure-parallel-heads": {"title": "Read side-by-side work separately from the depth of the layers.", "takeaway": "**Several heads contribute within one layer**, and then the result moves to the next layer. Counting heads and counting layers answer different questions about the design."}, "modern-figure-depth-state": {"title": "Follow a changing representation through repeated processing steps.", "takeaway": "**The output of one step becomes the input to a later step.** The link to physical dynamics is about this repeated change. The model's internal numbers do not literally move."}, "modern-figure-sequence-vectors": {"title": "Keep the representation separate from the next-item task.", "takeaway": "**The input items are represented as vectors, even when they come from different materials.** Context across the sequence decides which continuation the model should favor."}, "modern-figure-syllable-patterns": {"title": "Compare an allowed onset with a cluster split by a syllable boundary.", "takeaway": "**Position within the syllable changes which sequences are allowed.** A consonant cluster in the middle of a word does not prove that the same cluster can start a word."}, "modern-figure-letter-options": {"title": "Read limits on the next letter as information in the context.", "takeaway": "**Some beginnings allow many letters, while others allow only a few.** So their probability distributions should differ, because uncertain contexts are not all equally open."}, "modern-figure-agent-graph": {"title": "Separate the levels, from one neuron up to a graph of communicating models.", "takeaway": "**Processing inside a model is different from messages passed between models doing tasks.** The connection from critic to reviser belongs to the organization of the larger system."}}, "asked": {"three-modes": {"title": "Count the modes and explain how each one moves.", "items": [{"question": "How many normal modes does a three-mass system have when the masses move in one direction?", "answer": "A three-mass system has **three** normal modes. Each mass that can move on its own adds one **degree of freedom** (an independent way to move). So three masses give three separate mode patterns."}, {"question": "Which of the three modes has the highest frequency, and what makes it faster?", "answer": "**Mode 3** has the highest frequency. In Mode 3, neighboring masses move in opposite directions, so the springs stretch more. That gives a stronger pull back than the shared motion of Mode 1."}, {"question": "What happens to Mode 2 if only the middle mass is made heavier?", "answer": "Mode 2 does not change in the ideal symmetric system. **The middle mass does not move in Mode 2**, so its added inertia has no effect on that mode."}]}, "local-change": {"title": "Decide what matters at a marked location.", "items": [{"question": "A marked point moves strongly in one mode but stays still in another. Will added mass affect both modes equally?", "answer": "No, added mass affects the two modes differently. It lowers the frequency of the mode that moves a lot at that point. It has no effect on a mode with an exact node there, so **the effect depends on how much the point moves in each mode**."}, {"question": "Why can extra stiffness at a point that stays still change a mode's frequency?", "answer": "Extra stiffness can still matter because the point may sit where neighboring regions stretch strongly. Its own displacement can be zero while the material around it deforms. **Stiffness responds to stretching and compression**, not only to how far the point itself moves."}, {"question": "If the same change raises one resonance and lowers another, must the measurements be inconsistent?", "answer": "No, the measurements can be perfectly consistent. The changed location can be dominated by compression in one mode and by motion in the other. **Those different roles allow the frequencies to shift in opposite directions.**"}]}, "fixed-fixed": {"title": "Fit the end conditions before naming the frequencies.", "items": [{"question": "A uniform string is fixed at both ends and has a 200 Hz fundamental. What frequency is its fifth mode?", "answer": "The fifth mode is at **1,000 Hz**. An ideal string fixed at both ends has whole-number harmonics. So its fifth mode is five times the 200 Hz fundamental."}, {"question": "Why does the second mode of a string fixed at both ends have a shorter wavelength than the first?", "answer": "The second mode fits two half-waves into the length that held one. **More wave pieces in the same length means a shorter wavelength.** At the same wave speed, a shorter wavelength gives a higher frequency."}, {"question": "Which frequencies appear in a spectrum of the first six modes when the fundamental is 200 Hz?", "answer": "The spectrum has peaks at **200, 400, 600, 800, 1,000 and 1,200 Hz**. These are whole-number multiples of the fundamental, so the peaks are evenly spaced 200 Hz apart."}]}, "fixed-free": {"title": "Explain why a fixed end and a free end allow only odd multiples.", "items": [{"question": "What would go wrong if an even number of quarter-waves were fitted between a fixed end and a free end?", "answer": "An even number of quarter-waves would put a still point at the free end. The free end must have the greatest displacement, so **the end conditions would no longer match**."}, {"question": "Why does a building help explain the end conditions of a vocal tract?", "answer": "A building helps because it is held at its base and free at its top. A neutral vocal-tract air column also has different conditions at the glottis end and the lip end. So the two share a **fixed-free arrangement**."}, {"question": "For an ideal fixed-free tube with a 500 Hz first resonance, what are the next two resonances?", "answer": "The next two resonances are **1,500 Hz and 2,500 Hz**. An ideal neutral tube has resonances at odd multiples of its lowest resonance. So 500 Hz is followed by three times and five times 500 Hz."}]}, "source": {"title": "Keep the fundamental and its harmonics separate.", "items": [{"question": "A periodic voice has F0 at 150 Hz. Where are its first five harmonic peaks?", "answer": "The first five harmonics are at **150, 300, 450, 600 and 750 Hz**. Harmonics fall at whole-number multiples of how often the source repeats. So each one is 150 Hz above the one before."}, {"question": "What happens to harmonic spacing if the vocal folds vibrate faster?", "answer": "**The spacing gets wider** when the vocal folds vibrate faster. F0 is the repetition rate, and it is also the gap between neighboring harmonics. So a higher F0 means a bigger gap."}, {"question": "Does the third harmonic have to be the third formant?", "answer": "No, the third harmonic does not have to be the third formant. **A harmonic belongs to the repeating source**, while **a formant is a resonance of the tract**. Their numbers count different things."}]}, "constriction": {"title": "Work out a constriction's effect from the mode pattern.", "items": [{"question": "A constriction raises F2 strongly. Was the air mostly moving or compressing at that location in Mode 2?", "answer": "**Compression was the stronger effect** at that location. Narrowing adds stiffness where the mode compresses, so the air pushes back harder and the frequency rises."}, {"question": "Why does narrowing the tract not always raise every formant?", "answer": "Narrowing does not raise every formant because it also adds inertia. **At a place where the air moves strongly, the added inertia can win and lower a formant.** This can happen even though stiffness also increases there."}, {"question": "What extra information is needed before predicting whether a local tract change raises F1?", "answer": "You need to know **where the change sits in the first mode's pattern of motion and compression**. Knowing only that a narrowing happened does not tell you which way F1 moves."}]}, "i": {"title": "Explain why F1 and F2 move in opposite directions.", "items": [{"question": "How can one front constriction lower F1 while raising F2?", "answer": "The front constriction sits where the first mode mainly moves and the second mode mainly compresses. **Added inertia lowers F1, and added stiffness raises F2.**"}, {"question": "Which vowel change lowers both the first and second formants, and why?", "answer": "The **/u/ model** lowers both of the first two formants. It narrows the tract near the lips, where both modes move strongly. So the added inertia lowers both frequencies."}, {"question": "Why does moving a constriction toward the /a/ location reverse the /i/ pattern?", "answer": "The pattern reverses because the first two modes play different roles at the new location. **Compression dominates Mode 1, and motion dominates Mode 2.** So F1 rises and F2 falls."}]}, "filter": {"title": "Describe what the source supplies and what the filter changes.", "items": [{"question": "What changes when the same voiced source is filtered as /i/ and then /a/?", "answer": "**The formant pattern and the relative heights of the harmonics** change as the tract shape changes. The spacing of the source harmonics can stay the same."}, {"question": "Why can a formant be present even when no harmonic lies exactly at its frequency?", "answer": "A formant can exist without a harmonic on it because it belongs to the tract's resonant response. **The source only samples that response at its harmonic frequencies.** So a resonance does not have to line up with a harmonic."}, {"question": "Why can high-pitched singing make a vowel harder to identify?", "answer": "High-pitched singing can hide a vowel because a high F0 spreads the harmonics far apart. Fewer peaks then sample the formant envelope. So the vowel's resonance pattern becomes less clear."}]}, "spectrogram": {"title": "Read what a spectrogram shows.", "items": [{"question": "What does a dark region high on a spectrogram represent?", "answer": "A dark region high up shows **strong energy at a high frequency** at that moment. On a spectrogram, height shows frequency and darkness shows amplitude."}, {"question": "How would a transition from /i/ toward /a/ appear in the first two formant bands?", "answer": "**F1 would rise and F2 would fall** as the vowel moves toward /a/. The bands would shift up or down over time. They would not just get darker in the same place."}, {"question": "What information does a spectrum alone leave out that a spectrogram provides?", "answer": "A spectrum leaves out how the sound changes over time, because it describes only one time window. **A spectrogram keeps the order of the changing frequency patterns**, so transitions can be followed."}]}, "backward": {"title": "Explain the order of the backward calculation.", "items": [{"question": "Why is the effect of a final-layer weight easier to connect to the error than the effect of an early weight?", "answer": "A final-layer weight is easier to connect because it has a short, direct path to the output. An early weight affects the error **through several later activations and connections**. So its effect depends on everything that comes after it."}, {"question": "How does dynamic programming help backpropagation avoid repeated work?", "answer": "Dynamic programming lets backpropagation solve the later error calculations first and then reuse them. **Shared calculations do not have to be rebuilt separately for each weight**, so a great deal of repeated work is avoided."}, {"question": "Does calculating a gradient itself complete an SGD weight update?", "answer": "No, calculating a gradient does not finish the update. **Backpropagation calculates the gradient.** The optimizer then changes the weight using that gradient and the learning rate."}]}, "weight-direction": {"title": "Trace a proposed weight change to its result.", "items": [{"question": "A positive hidden activation feeds an output that already has too much support. What does increasing its direct output weight do?", "answer": "Increasing the weight raises that output's raw score, which makes its extra support worse. A positive activation times a larger weight gives a larger contribution. **The sign of the incoming activation** makes this direction predictable."}, {"question": "Would increasing the same connection necessarily have the same effect if its input activation were negative?", "answer": "No, the effect would be different with a negative activation. A negative activation times a larger weight pushes the contribution the other way. **The sign of the input must be considered along with the weight.**"}, {"question": "Why should an early hidden weight not be judged only from the sign printed beside it?", "answer": "An early weight's final effect passes through later connections and nonlinear steps. **Those later paths decide how the change reaches the error.** So the sign beside the weight is not enough."}]}, "sequential": {"title": "Read the layer sizes and change the network so they still match.", "items": [{"question": "How many hidden layers are in a network with processing sizes 3, 2, 4 and 5, where the five neurons are the output?", "answer": "The network has **three hidden layers**. The final five-neuron layer is the output layer. The two input values are not another hidden layer."}, {"question": "What does nn.Linear(3, 2) specify?", "answer": "The layer nn.Linear(3, 2) takes in **three values** and produces **two activations**. Each activation is a learned weighted combination of the three inputs. So the next processing layer must accept two inputs."}, {"question": "Which two connections are needed to insert three hidden neurons between four hidden activations and five outputs?", "answer": "You need a connection from **four to three**, followed by a connection from **three to five**. This adds the new hidden layer and keeps the five output categories."}]}, "training-code": {"title": "Recognize what each training step does.", "items": [{"question": "Why must the row order agree between formant inputs and vowel targets?", "answer": "The row order must match because each input row and its target row describe the same recording. **A mismatched row teaches the wrong formant-to-vowel pairing**, even if both tables contain valid numbers."}, {"question": "What does loss.backward() contribute before the optimizer takes a step?", "answer": "The call loss.backward() computes how the loss depends on each trainable weight. **These gradients give the direction and size of each update.** The optimizer's step then uses them."}, {"question": "What is the effect of increasing the learning rate while leaving the gradients unchanged?", "answer": "**The weight steps get larger** when the learning rate goes up. Larger steps can speed up learning. They can also make training less stable, with more ups and downs."}]}, "sigmoid": {"title": "Explain the S-shaped transformation without calculating exponentials.", "items": [{"question": "What does sigmoid do to a very negative weighted sum?", "answer": "Sigmoid turns a very negative weighted sum into an activation **close to zero**. A very positive sum gives a value close to one."}, {"question": "Why does sigmoid change the function of a stack of linear layers?", "answer": "Sigmoid adds a nonlinear step between the weighted sums. **So the network can no longer be collapsed into one linear transformation** in the same way."}, {"question": "Why are three hidden sigmoid values not automatically probabilities over three categories?", "answer": "Each value lies between zero and one, but **the three values do not have to add up to one**. Being in the probability range is only one rule of a probability distribution."}]}, "accuracy": {"title": "Interpret the training curve and the repeated runs.", "items": [{"question": "Why is twenty percent a useful guessing baseline for five equally likely categories?", "answer": "A random guess has **one chance in five** of picking the right category. So twenty percent is the expected score from guessing alone. The baseline comes from having five equally likely choices."}, {"question": "Does a flat training-accuracy curve mean that every example is classified correctly?", "answer": "No, a flat curve does not mean every example is correct. It only means the share correct has stopped rising. **A steady result can still include many wrong decisions.**"}, {"question": "What remains different when runs start with different weights but finish at the same accuracy?", "answer": "**Their learning paths can still differ.** The early predictions and corrections depend on the starting weights. This holds even when the final share correct is the same."}]}, "next-token": {"title": "Apply the sequence-prediction idea to different materials.", "items": [{"question": "What do words and a sequence of acoustic measurements have in common for a numerical predictor?", "answer": "Both can be represented as **ordered vectors**. The model uses the earlier vectors to predict what comes next. The representation and the meaning of the output still differ between the two."}, {"question": "Why can an isolated word be insufficient to decide the next word?", "answer": "One word alone may not be enough because its continuation depends on the wider sequence. **Earlier context changes which next items fit.** So classifying each item separately leaves out useful information."}, {"question": "What does a recurrent connection contribute to a sequence model?", "answer": "A recurrent connection carries state from one processing step to the next. **So the current calculation can depend on earlier context**, not only on the current input."}]}, "phonotactics": {"title": "Use a word beginning to reason about what comes next.", "items": [{"question": "Why is n a poor next-letter choice after initial sp in this English pattern exercise?", "answer": "The letter n is a poor choice because it would make the **spn onset**, which this English pattern does not allow. A vowel, l or r fits instead."}, {"question": "Why can a consonant sequence occur inside a longer word but fail as a word beginning?", "answer": "A syllable break can split the consonants into different syllables. **Allowed sequences depend on syllable structure.** So a cluster that crosses a syllable boundary does not have to be an allowed onset."}, {"question": "How should a predictor's distribution differ between a highly restricted beginning and a permissive one?", "answer": "After a very limited beginning, the predictor should put its support on a few continuations. After an open beginning, it should spread a **broader distribution** over many possible next items."}]}, "heads": {"title": "Separate the parts inside a model from a system of models.", "items": [{"question": "Are several attention heads in one layer the same as several successive processing layers?", "answer": "No, heads and layers are different. **Heads work side by side within one layer**, while layers transform information one after another."}, {"question": "Why is a critic-to-reviser loop a kind of feedback?", "answer": "The loop is feedback because the critic's evaluation goes back and changes the next attempt. **The later result depends on feedback about an earlier result.**"}, {"question": "Does calling a head an expert guarantee that it understands a topic as a person does?", "answer": "No, calling a head an expert does not mean it understands like a person. The term describes a specialized calculating contribution. It does not show a human-like role or an area of expertise that can be checked separately."}]}}, "review": {"1": {"answer": "A system with several masses has several frequencies because it can vibrate in several different ways, called **modes**. In each mode the masses move together or against each other in a different pattern, so each mode stretches the springs by a different amount. When the masses move against each other, the springs between them stretch and compress more, so more stiffness is working and that mode vibrates faster. So each mode has its own frequency, and a system with N masses has **N modes and N frequencies**.", "teaching": ["In a two-mass system, two masses sit between two walls and are joined by three springs. A **mode** is one way of vibrating, and the two masses can move **together** or **against each other**, which gives two different modes.", "When the two masses move together, the spring between them moves along with them and never stretches, so only the two outer springs are working. When the masses move against each other, the middle spring stretches and compresses too, so all three springs are working.", "**More working springs give more stiffness, and more stiffness gives a higher frequency.** That is why the two modes have different frequencies, even though the masses and springs themselves never change."], "change": "He could ask the same question about a membrane such as the vocal folds. He could also ask why the highest mode is the fastest. The reasoning stays the same, so name the modes and explain how much the springs stretch in each one.", "question": "Why do multiple mass systems have multiple frequencies?"}, "2": {"answer": "A 3-mass system has **three modes**. This is because a system with N masses has N modes of vibration.", "teaching": ["A chain of masses joined by springs can vibrate in as many ways as it has masses, so **N masses give N modes**. One mass has one mode, two masses have two modes and three masses have three modes.", "Each new mass adds one more pattern of moving together or against its neighbors, so each new mass adds exactly **one more mode**."], "change": "He could give a different number of masses. Four masses give four modes, and six masses give six modes.", "question": "How many modes does a 3-mass system have?"}, "3": {"answer": "**Mode 3 has the highest frequency.** In Mode 3 every mass moves against its neighbor, so every spring stretches and compresses as much as possible. That puts the most stiffness to work, and more stiffness pulls the masses back to the middle faster, so Mode 3 vibrates fastest.", "teaching": ["In a 3-mass system, three masses are joined by four springs. In **Mode 1**, all three masses move **together**, so the middle springs hardly stretch and the frequency is the **lowest**.", "In **Mode 2**, the **middle mass stays still** while the two outer masses move against each other. The springs stretch more than in Mode 1, so the frequency is **higher**.", "In **Mode 3**, the middle mass moves against both outer masses, so **every mass moves against its neighbor**. This mode has the most stretching and compression, so it has the **highest frequency**."], "change": "He could ask which mode has the lowest frequency, and that is Mode 1 because the masses move together. He could also ask about a 4-mass system. The highest mode is always the one where every mass moves against its neighbors.", "question": "Which mode of the 3-mass system has the highest frequency? Why?"}, "4": {"answer": "**Mode 1 and Mode 3 go down, and Mode 2 stays the same.** The middle mass moves in Mode 1 and in Mode 3, so its extra weight slows the system and lowers the frequency of both modes. In Mode 2 the middle mass stays still, so its extra weight has **no effect** on that frequency.", "teaching": ["In a 3-mass system, the middle mass is called **Mass 2**. **A mass only changes the frequency of a mode in which that mass moves**, so extra weight on a mass that stays still changes nothing, however heavy it gets.", "The answer depends on what Mass 2 does in each mode. Mass 2 **moves in Mode 1 and Mode 3**, so those frequencies go down. Mass 2 **stays still in Mode 2**, so that frequency does not change.", "**Perturbation theory** predicts how a small change in one location affects how something vibrates. The same change at one spot affects each mode differently, because the spot moves in some modes and stays still in others."], "change": "He could ask about stiffening a spring instead of adding mass. A spring only changes the frequency of a mode in which that spring stretches, so stiffening a spring that does not stretch in a mode does nothing to that mode. Stiffening a spring that stretches raises that mode's frequency.", "question": "If you increase mass 2, what happens to the frequencies of each of the modes?"}, "5": {"answer": "An infinite number of masses and springs gives a **string**, such as a guitar or piano string. Every small piece of a string has mass, and every small piece acts as a spring that stretches and compresses. So a string vibrates in modes just like a chain of masses, only with an infinite number of modes.", "teaching": ["A chain of masses and springs turns into a **string** when more and more masses and springs are added, all the way to an **infinite number**.", "A surface built the same way, such as a **drum** or the **vocal folds**, is called a **membrane**. A membrane is also an infinite number of masses and springs."], "change": "He could ask what a drum or the vocal folds are in these terms. Both are membranes, which are made of an infinite number of masses and springs.", "question": "What do you get when you have an infinite number of masses and springs?"}, "6": {"answer": "Circle the four points inside the string where it crosses its middle line, along with the two fixed ends. At those points the string does not move, but the pieces on each side move in opposite directions. So the string is stretched and compressed the most there, which means those points have the **most springiness**. The tops of the bumps move the most, so they have the least springiness.", "teaching": ["Along a vibrating string, **the places with the most movement have the least stretching, and the places with the most stretching have the least movement**. So motion and springiness alternate along the string.", "The **peaks of the bumps** move up and down the most, so they act as mass. The **still points** between the bumps and at the fixed ends do not move, so they are where the springs work hardest.", "**Mode 5** of a string fixed at both ends has five bumps. So it has four still points inside the string, plus the two fixed ends."], "change": "He could show a different mode. Mode N of a string fixed at both ends has N bumps and N minus one still points inside the string. The springiness is always at the still points and at the two ends.", "question": "This is Mode 5 of a string. Circle the areas where there is most springiness."}, "7": {"answer": "**Mode 5 has a frequency of 1000 Hz.** In a string fixed at both ends, each mode is a whole-number multiple of the base frequency. So Mode 5 is five times 200 Hz.", "teaching": ["The **base frequency** is the frequency of Mode 1. In a system **fixed at both ends**, Mode 2 is twice the base frequency, Mode 3 is three times the base frequency, and so on.", "The arithmetic stays simple, because the frequency of a mode is just the mode number times the base frequency."], "change": "He could give a different base frequency or a different mode. He could also switch to a system fixed at one end and free at the other. Then only odd multiples occur, so Mode 2 is three times the base frequency and Mode 3 is five times the base frequency.", "question": "The string above is fixed on both sides. If mode 1 has frequency 200 Hz, what frequency does Mode 5 have?"}, "8": {"answer": "The frequencies are evenly spaced whole-number multiples of the base frequency because each higher mode fits a whole number of copies of the first mode into the same length. The first mode is half a wave that goes from no motion at one end to the most motion in the middle and back to no motion. Both ends must stay still, so every mode has to fit a whole number of these half waves. Mode 2 fits two, so it vibrates **twice as fast**, and Mode 3 fits three, so it vibrates **three times as fast**.", "teaching": ["In a system **fixed at both ends**, the multiples come from **counting how many copies of the first mode fit** into each higher mode.", "More pieces in the same length make **shorter waves**, so the vibration is faster. So the frequency grows by one base frequency with each new mode.", "These evenly spaced frequencies are called **harmonics**. In speech, the vocal folds are the main system fixed at its edges."], "change": "He could ask the same question about a system fixed at one end and free at the other. That system gives odd multiples instead, because its modes are built from quarter-wave pieces that must end with motion at the free end.", "question": "Why are the frequencies of fixed-fixed vibratory systems spaced the way they are?"}, "9": {"answer": "Draw a frequency axis along the bottom and an amplitude axis going up. Then draw **six evenly spaced spikes at 200, 400, 600, 800, 1000 and 1200 Hz**, one for each mode. If each mode is equally strong, the spikes can be the same height.", "teaching": ["A **spectrum** shows **which frequencies a vibration has and how much amplitude (strength) each one has**. Frequency runs along one axis, and amplitude runs along the other.", "A string fixed at both ends has every whole-number multiple of the base frequency. So its spikes are **evenly spaced**, one base frequency apart."], "change": "He could ask for a system fixed at one end and free at the other. Then the spikes fall only at odd multiples, so with a base of 200 Hz they sit at 200, 600, 1000 and 1400 Hz.", "question": "Draw the spectrum for the string above up to mode 6."}, "10": {"answer": "Draw spikes at **150, 300, 450, 600 and 750 Hz**, with frequency along the bottom and amplitude going up. The vocal folds are a membrane fixed on its edges, so their harmonics are whole-number multiples of F0. So the spikes are evenly spaced, 150 Hz apart.", "teaching": ["**F0** is the base frequency of the vocal folds, which is the frequency of their lowest mode.", "The vocal folds are **fixed on their edges**, so they follow the fixed-fixed rule and give **evenly spaced harmonics**. Measurements of real voices in Praat, a program for analyzing speech, show the second harmonic at about double the first.", "**Heavier, looser folds give a lower F0**, so their harmonics are closer together. **Tighter, lighter folds give a higher F0**, so their harmonics are farther apart."], "change": "He could give a different F0, and then each spike is a whole-number multiple of that value. He could also ask what happens to the spacing when the folds get tighter or heavier. Tighter folds spread the harmonics apart, and heavier folds pack them closer together.", "question": "If vocal folds have their base frequency, F0, at 150, draw the spectrum for the first 5 harmonics."}, "11": {"answer": "Circle the fixed bottom and the three points where the building crosses its resting position. Those points do not move, but the parts above and below them move in opposite directions. So the structure stretches and compresses the most there, which means those points have the **most springiness**. The free top moves the most and has the least springiness, so it is not circled.", "teaching": ["A building is **fixed at the ground and free at the top**, so it is a **fixed-free system**.", "In every mode of a fixed-free system, the **fixed end always has springiness**, and the **free end always has the most motion**.", "**Mode 4** holds **seven quarter-wave pieces**. Each piece goes from no motion to the most motion, so between the fixed bottom and the free top there are three more still points where the springiness is greatest."], "change": "He could show Mode 2 or Mode 3 instead. Mode 2 has one still point above the base, and Mode 3 has two still points above the base. In every mode the base is springy and the top is moving.", "question": "This picture shows mode 4 of a building. Circle the areas where there is the most springiness."}, "12": {"answer": "The building is analogous to **the air in the vocal tract**. The air in the vocal tract is fixed at one end and free at the other, just as the building is.", "teaching": ["The air in the vocal tract is **fixed at the vocal folds**, because the closed folds keep the air there from moving. It is **free at the lips**, because the air there moves in and out easily.", "The frequencies of vibration of this air are the **formants**, and the formants make the vowels and consonants of speech."], "change": "He could ask the reverse, which is what in the physical world behaves like the air in the vocal tract. The answer is a building, or a string fixed at one end and free at the other.", "question": "What in speech is this building analogous to?"}, "13": {"answer": "The analogy holds because **both are fixed at one end and free at the other**. A building is fixed to the ground and free to sway at the top. The air in the vocal tract is fixed at the closed vocal folds and free to move at the open lips. Because the ends are held the same way, both have the same kind of modes, with springiness at the fixed end, the most motion at the free end and frequencies at odd multiples of the first.", "teaching": ["Two systems behave alike when they are made of **masses and springs held the same way at their ends**. The materials do not matter, only how each end is held.", "**Mode 1** of the air in the vocal tract matches Mode 1 of a swaying building, because both have **no motion at the fixed end and the most motion at the free end**."], "change": "He could ask how the building and the air differ. Engineers design buildings to sway only in Mode 1, but the air in the vocal tract vibrates in all of its modes at once.", "question": "Why does this analogy hold?"}, "14": {"answer": "**Mode 3 goes up and Mode 4 goes down.** In Mode 3 the boxed spot does not move but is stretched the most, so the added mass does nothing and the added stiffness raises the frequency. In Mode 4 the boxed spot moves the most and is not stretched, so the added mass lowers the frequency and the added stiffness does nothing.", "teaching": ["In a system with many masses, raising the mass and the stiffness together does **not** cancel out. This is because **where** the change happens decides which effect counts in each mode.", "At a **still point**, only the stiffness matters, so **the frequency goes up**. At a **moving point**, only the mass matters, so **the frequency goes down**.", "Each mode has to be checked separately, because the same spot can be still in one mode and moving in another."], "change": "He could move the box or ask about different modes. For each mode, check whether the box sits on a still point or on a peak, and then apply the rule. A still point means the frequency goes up, and a peak means it goes down.", "question": "If we increase the stiffness and mass of the string at the location in box, what happened to Mode 3 and Mode 4's frequencies and why?"}, "15": {"answer": "**The frequency of Mode 4 goes down.** The top of the building is the free end, so it has the most motion and no springiness. The added mass counts because the top is moving a lot, so it lowers the frequency. The added stiffness does nothing because nothing is stretching there.", "teaching": ["The free end of a fixed-free system **moves the most in every mode**, so a change there acts only through **mass**.", "The same thing happens in the vowel /u/. Rounding the lips adds mass at the free end of the vocal tract, where the air moves the most, so the formants go down."], "change": "He could ask about adding mass and stiffness at the very bottom instead. The base never moves but is always springy, so the added stiffness counts and the frequency goes up.", "question": "If we increase the mass and stiffness of the building at its very top, what would happen to the frequency of vibration of mode 4, and why?"}, "16": {"answer": "The formants are odd multiples because a fixed-free system can only hold an **odd number of quarter-wave pieces**. The basic piece runs from no motion at the fixed end to the most motion at the free end, which is a quarter of a wave. Every mode must start still at the fixed end and end with the most motion at the free end. Adding one more piece would leave a still point at the free end, which is not allowed, so pieces are added two at a time. That gives one, three, five and seven pieces, so the formants are 1, 3, 5 and 7 times the base frequency.", "teaching": ["In a system **fixed at both ends**, the basic piece goes from still to moving and back to still. Both ends are the same, so every whole number of pieces fits.", "In a **fixed-free system**, the two ends are different, so **only odd numbers of quarter-wave pieces fit**. So the frequencies jump from one to three to five times the base frequency."], "change": "He could give a base frequency, such as 500 Hz, and ask for the next two formants. They are 1500 Hz and 2500 Hz, not 1000 Hz and 1500 Hz.", "question": "Why are the formants of fixed-free system 1,3,5 etc. multiples of the base frequency?"}, "17": {"answer": "The **formants** of the air in the vocal tract have these properties. The air is fixed at the vocal folds and free at the lips, so its frequencies of vibration are odd multiples of the first formant.", "teaching": ["**Formants are the frequencies of vibration of a system fixed on one side and completely free on the other.** In speech, that system is the **air in the vocal tract**.", "In **schwa**, the vocal tract has no constriction (narrowing), so the formants sit at evenly spaced odd multiples. Every other vowel shifts the formants by making a constriction somewhere in the tract."], "change": "He could ask what aspect of speech has whole-number multiples instead. The answer is the harmonics of the vocal folds, because the folds are fixed on their edges.", "question": "What aspect of speech has the properties in 16?"}, "18": {"answer": "A **spectrogram** shows many spectra lined up one after another over time. Time runs along the horizontal axis, frequency runs up the vertical axis, and darkness shows how much amplitude there is at each frequency and time. The **formants show up as dark bands**, so you can follow them rising and falling as the speaker changes sounds. For example, from /i/ to /a/ the F1 band rises and the F2 band falls.", "teaching": ["A **spectrum shows one instant**, while a **spectrogram shows how the spectrum changes over time**.", "On a spectrogram, black means high amplitude, gray means medium amplitude and white means low amplitude.", "Speech is never one steady vowel, so the formants are always moving. A spectrogram makes that movement visible."], "change": "He could ask how a spectrum and a spectrogram differ. A spectrum shows one moment, and a spectrogram shows many moments over time. He could also ask what the dark bands are, and they are the formants.", "question": "Describe a spectrogram."}, "19": {"answer": "At that location the air must be **very springy and barely moving** in that mode. A constriction raises both the mass and the stiffness of the air there. The formant went up a great deal, so the added stiffness must be what counted. Stiffness only counts where the air compresses and expands, and the added mass did nothing because the air there was not moving.", "teaching": ["A **constriction** (narrowing) of the vocal tract always raises both the mass and the stiffness of the air there. **Compressing air makes it stiffer**, and packing more air into one spot makes it heavier.", "Which effect counts depends on **whether the air in that mode is moving or compressing at the constriction**. If the air is compressing there, the formant goes up. If the air is moving there, the formant goes down.", "In /i/, the constriction two thirds of the way up the tract is at a moving spot for Mode 1 and a springy spot for Mode 2. So **F1 goes down and F2 goes up**."], "change": "He could ask about the opposite case, a constriction that lowers a formant a great deal. That means the air there is moving a lot and barely compressing, as at the lips for /u/.", "question": "If I constrict the vocal tract somewhere and I raise the formant a great deal, what must be true of air motion vs. springiness at this location?"}, "20": {"answer": "The network has **three hidden layers**, with three, two and four neurons. It has five layers in all, which are the input layer with two neurons, the three hidden layers and the output layer with five neurons. There are only four Linear lines, because the input layer does no calculations and so gets no line of code.", "teaching": ["In PyTorch, each **nn.Linear** line describes one layer that does calculations. Its first number is how many values come in, and its second number is how many neurons the layer has.", "The second number of the **last line** is the **output layer**. So the hidden layers are the second numbers of every line except the last.", "Each layer's number of outputs must match the next layer's number of inputs, so the numbers can pass all the way through the network."], "change": "He could give a longer or shorter Sequential, or one with nn.Sigmoid lines in between. Sigmoid lines are not layers, so count only the Linear lines. He could also ask how many layers there are in total, and then the input layer counts too.", "question": "How many hidden layers does this NN have?"}, "21": {"answer": "Change the old last line from 4 in and 5 out to **4 in and 3 out**, and then add a new last line that takes **3 in and gives 5 out**. The first three lines stay the same. The new hidden layer of three neurons now sits right before the output layer, and every layer takes in exactly as many numbers as the layer before it gives out.", "teaching": ["Adding a layer means adding one Linear line and **fixing the sizes on both sides of it**. So the line before the new layer must output three numbers, and the new line must take in three numbers.", "Writing PyTorch from scratch and getting every dot and bracket right is not the skill being tested. Changing the layer sizes so they match is the skill being tested."], "change": "He could ask for an extra layer right after the input, or for a new layer with a different number of neurons. In every case, the numbers must match where one line meets the next.", "question": "Write the code for an NN with an additional hidden layer right before the output layer with 3 neurons, and adjust the other layers."}}};
